insert into public.chunks (id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length)
select id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length
from jsonb_to_recordset($jsonpayload$
[{"id": "81cb70b4-a743-46a4-a0af-693723c3cdcf", "episode_id": "fde769da-fa02-42e1-88b0-86f7ca00aa72", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4863", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "John Salzinger (00:46:56): The problem is not recognizing what you just said. So awareness is key. If you're looking at the news as an example, you should make sure you understand what's accurate, real and what is likely bs. You should research your sources. Same thing with what you're talking about. I think it's important to understand you're being targeted. Oh, I didn't just get some ad that I'm interested in magically, maybe automagically it happened because you've been studied, you've signed onto the terms and conditions, and you've given away a lot of your privacy for the convenience of being able to be served ads that are useful to you. So the question at the end of the day is do you leave those settings on? Do you know that there's settings like that on with advertisers that you can turn off the targeting, that you can leave on the targeting? Is it useful for you? (00:47:57): If it is, great, but do you understand what's happening? Do you know how much of your information is going out there? It's about educating yourself. The information is out there. Unfortunately, this sort of education is not what advertisers are pushing. It's not what the political structure is pushing, right? This is sort of hidden secrets, but it's tough because the inequity and education access to media, the monopolies now that kind of control this, whether it's a few advertising firms that own them all, or a few media companies that own all the news. It's a difficult, it's easy for me to say it. I recognize it because I've been taught it because I've been lucky in my life to be born in New York City with progressive parents, had a good education. I'm lucky, simple, nothing else. And so how do we get that information to others? I think advertising advertisers have a responsibility to be more transparent, to question on their practices and give people more choice.", "token_estimate": 469, "speakers": ["John Salzinger"], "start_timestamp": "00:46:56", "end_timestamp": "00:46:56", "podcast_index": 56, "episode_name": "EP 56: John Salzinger | The Advertisers Responsibility | MPOWERD", "slug": "ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "guest_name": "John Salzinger", "guest_title": "Founder of MPOWERD", "categories": [], "short_description": "Toxic consumerism, advertising and the marketer's responsibility", "podcast_length": "1:20:40"}, {"id": "28fcc4c4-4203-481d-bf0e-08ccfa5ee0b6", "episode_id": "fde769da-fa02-42e1-88b0-86f7ca00aa72", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4863", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:49:03): Sure. Okay. So it sounds like you're saying two things and to pick 'em apart, one of 'em is it's the consumer's job to be educated about how advertisers are trying to communicate with them. Now, that gets into, as you just said, a discussion about the quality of education and people's ability to learn that in the first place. But on the other end, advertisers, they already know how they're doing it. So is there a moral obligation there? Or because they know what they're doing, they can say, yes, we're going to do this. No, we're not going to do this. If not all the general public is educated, then do advertisers have an obligation to kind of make that decision for them?\n\nJohn Salzinger (00:49:53): I mean, morally, yeah, but they're not going to, right? So you look at, I don't know, I guess is it the FCC or, I mean there's a lot of false information in advertising that's pushed on consumers, and it's deregulated at this point. I mean, literally you look at Instagram, you look at Amazon. I mean, the amount of claims that are made that are false on products that are not regulated have never been larger. And across the board, I think there's enough public sector scrutinization or certification or even consumer reports that people, it's not where it used to be. People just have kind of gotten won over and complacent. And it's not until you get that product that you realize doesn't work the way it's said. This filter's not taking lead out. I mean, whatever it is. I mean, it's a scary thing. Yes, there's a moral obligation. (00:50:57): The solution is policy. I think it is something that governments should regulate. I think consumers and the public should be protected from false advertising. But it's hard. I mean, you can look at political ads and that same structure gives you false advertising on their candidates, and they get into power and do totally different things. So even on their rep. So it's a tough one. But yeah, of course they have a moral obligation and we have to speak out This podcast that you're doing, the subjects that you are entertaining is helping, but everyone can help in their own way.", "token_estimate": 541, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "John Salzinger"], "start_timestamp": "00:49:03", "end_timestamp": "00:49:53", "podcast_index": 56, "episode_name": "EP 56: John Salzinger | The Advertisers Responsibility | MPOWERD", "slug": "ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "guest_name": "John Salzinger", "guest_title": "Founder of MPOWERD", "categories": [], "short_description": "Toxic consumerism, advertising and the marketer's responsibility", "podcast_length": "1:20:40"}, {"id": "87f2bef2-3d8d-40f5-9739-b26aa558427f", "episode_id": "fde769da-fa02-42e1-88b0-86f7ca00aa72", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4863", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:51:39): So if the consumer is able to push the corporation to do good, how can consumers push the ad industry to, I don't know, be more ethical or do things in a better way? Are there ways,\n\nJohn Salzinger (00:51:58): I mean, the ad world, I guess is just, it's a tentacle of the corporations. It's a byproduct. It doesn't matter if you're a client or agent, you have a sole purpose. I think pushing the corporation thus pushes the ad agency or the internal advertising arm of the corporation. I don't think there's a distinction. Unfortunately. I think advertising agencies are run by corporations that are run by stock price. Again, and I don't mean to sound like someone that's so radical, but I do think when a society is making products for a casino, which is basically what the stock market is, you're betting not even on the present, but on futures, et cetera. I think there's a real obvious problem and disconnect between the public's interest. And if that wasn't there and companies were held accountable for what they're doing and the product they're offering, and consumers had a platform, maybe we need more platforms like Dun good to unveil these companies that don't do right, and advertisers that don't necessarily tell the truth. (00:53:23): I mean, pharma's a good example. You can look at advertisers that work for pharma. So there's this, I don't know if it's exact, but it's almost like an equal time scenario where they have to tell you all the side effects and amount of time you spend on side effects. You spend on the positives of the drug. One of the side effects might be death. I mean, I'm not kidding. I mean these ads are insane, yet the advertiser still has to sell the product. So what do they do while they're speaking really quickly or it's really fine print on all these horrible things that could happen to you. They have these wonderful images with soft focus and they're trying to distract you. And so the question is, is that the advertiser's fault? Well, I think it's the product actually. I think it's the corporation. And I think the advertiser got hired by the corporation to sell whatever they're offering. (00:54:14): So if the corporations aren't regulated, the advertisers are doing what they can to get a job, to get a client. So I don't think you can necessarily target the advertising agency or the technology behind it. It all boils down to what does the society as a whole post politically, economically on the corporate side and individually hold at the highest level? Are you looking at everyone should have a burger or are you looking at are we taking care of our elderly? Are we making it accessible for people to get education across the economic stratus? Are we doing the same thing in healthcare? And why not if we're not? And one example of this to me is when the fire department comes to your house, you don't ask for a credit card. I think there was a time when you may have had to pay for the fire department. No one thinks about that. Now, when the cops come, do you pay for the cops to come and help you? Hopefully there are good cops coming. There are a lot of good cops and there are some bad cops. But there are these catch words that are thrown out. And if you talk about advertising or the media or political parties, people throw out words like socialism, capitalism, and none of it's true. None of it's accurate. It's all much more complex. We're in a capitalist socialist society already. (00:55:51): And if you don't think we are, then how do you think we have highways? It just doesn't make any sense. And the problem there is it's not the messenger, it's the behind the scenes. It's the status quo. It's the amount of power that has been pushed into this small group of corporations, political parties, et cetera. And none of it has the interest of the consumer and the advertiser. The advertiser is the bridge. That's all it is. It's the bridge. The will do anything the company or political party tells it to do. Anything that makes money for that organization, it will do. And so if we don't look at news that's just media entertainment, and we only look at news, that's investigative, the news will change because the corporations will then want to spend money where the people are and the advertisers will be a bridge to good. So I don't see it as advertisers fault. I don't think they have any play in it, frankly.", "token_estimate": 1104, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "John Salzinger"], "start_timestamp": "00:51:39", "end_timestamp": "00:51:58", "podcast_index": 56, "episode_name": "EP 56: John Salzinger | The Advertisers Responsibility | MPOWERD", "slug": "ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "guest_name": "John Salzinger", "guest_title": "Founder of MPOWERD", "categories": [], "short_description": "Toxic consumerism, advertising and the marketer's responsibility", "podcast_length": "1:20:40"}, {"id": "0ddd1f98-72bc-4c24-a285-949b70ec4010", "episode_id": "fde769da-fa02-42e1-88b0-86f7ca00aa72", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4863", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:56:53): Interesting. Yeah, no, that's an interesting idea. So if corporations, as you're saying, don't have the public's interest at heart, generally speaking, why are they successful? Because isn't that how this is all supposed to work? Businesses tailored towards, again, coming back to that need versus once solving problems, that's how they succeed. How did we reach a place where they can not be serving the public's interest and still be succeeding?\n\nJohn Salzinger (00:57:25): I mean, the game is rigged. The corporations make money. They don't make money off serving the most people at a reasonable price. They make the most money by having the highest margins and the highest prices and the lowest cost of goods, even if it hurts workers in other countries. And it becomes this sort of vicious cycle where the consumers, at the end of the day, if we want really low prices, we are the ones that are supporting that system. If we want to participate in the process and care about how our goods are made, where they're made, how it's affecting human beings and workers' rights, how it's affecting the environment, well then we have to buy differently. We have to consume less. We have to think about what we need and make the world better. But if we buy into this feverish consumerism, it's tough to see. It's tough to see that changing. (00:58:35): It's difficult to see that changing. It does boil down to us. We have power. There's no corporation that works in a bubble even though they burn, but they don't. We have the ability to make sound decisions that have morality. And I think if we took our own morals that we have in our own family, or we might call it a pod these days, sadly, but if we took the morals that we embody in our own small group of people and push them out to society as a whole, then we would be automatically forcing corporations to sell us things. We need to solve problems and to do. Right. And the same would happen in the political spectrum.", "token_estimate": 500, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "John Salzinger"], "start_timestamp": "00:56:53", "end_timestamp": "00:57:25", "podcast_index": 56, "episode_name": "EP 56: John Salzinger | The Advertisers Responsibility | MPOWERD", "slug": "ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "guest_name": "John Salzinger", "guest_title": "Founder of MPOWERD", "categories": [], "short_description": "Toxic consumerism, advertising and the marketer's responsibility", "podcast_length": "1:20:40"}, {"id": "09fb7aad-cae7-4e2c-a018-2d2ad9c0e228", "episode_id": "fde769da-fa02-42e1-88b0-86f7ca00aa72", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4863", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:59:20): Just imagine how much good could come out of that if you already have a corporation that's built up to do whatever they do. Imagine if they could just switch, I don't know, switch gears, pivot somehow, and start doing their business for good. That would be massive.\n\nJohn Salzinger (00:59:40): Yeah, I mean, almost any corporation can do what we do. So empower's business model is such that the more people buy at reasonable margins, the more people support us by just buying our goods, the more we are able to reduce margins in emerging markets and help other people. Any company can do that. Any company, it's the same products, it's just different margins. So they're hyper localized and they're effective tools for people that can use them to better their lives. Any company can use that model. You build capacity, you help people, and you do it through your consumers buying things they need. It's a wonderful circle, and it's powerful and it's appealing to the people that we speak to in both the consumer side and the business side. It's appealing to human beings to be able to do good, especially if they're also getting what they want. You do both, but you don't have to make the most money every single day and just plow through everyone and everything to do so. And I think it's, it's a societal morality play more than an advertising issue. Kyla, people love to blame technology. They like to blame advertisers. They even like to blame one corporation. At the end of the day, it's all a reflection of us, all of it.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:01:09): So the outdoor industry, I feel like is pretty, I don't know, good at having a social good component built into it that just kind of seems to be something that a lot of brands like yours care about in this space. What are things that our industry is doing well and what are things that our industry could do better of?", "token_estimate": 475, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "John Salzinger"], "start_timestamp": "00:59:20", "end_timestamp": "01:01:09", "podcast_index": 56, "episode_name": "EP 56: John Salzinger | The Advertisers Responsibility | MPOWERD", "slug": "ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "guest_name": "John Salzinger", "guest_title": "Founder of MPOWERD", "categories": [], "short_description": "Toxic consumerism, advertising and the marketer's responsibility", "podcast_length": "1:20:40"}, {"id": "b2fa4449-0cfd-46d2-8628-ad0a77550265", "episode_id": "fde769da-fa02-42e1-88b0-86f7ca00aa72", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4863", "chunk_index": 23, "content": "John Salzinger (01:01:31): I mean, the outdoor industry is so far ahead of so many industries, and I think part of that is because its consumers use the environment as their playground and they respect it. And it's across all political spectrums. Those who use the outdoors care about the outdoors. And so it's easier for outdoor companies to do the right thing to protect the national parks, to protect clean water and air. And I think, so the outdoor industry is well placed to act as a leader in this. Now, anyone who doesn't ski or hike still has to breathe the air, still has to drink clean water and still should care just as much about it. And so the outdoor industry, it's been easy, right? It's an easy one because there's a connection there, an obvious connection. It would be wonderful if other industries made those connections and some do. (01:02:38): But you look at the banking industry as an example, and it's gone into investing your money derivatives and combining what were generally consumer banks with investment houses. And what would be better is if banks went back to investing in their communities and small business loans in a material way. In working with microfinance organizations in underdeveloped world situations, there's plenty that can be done with that money. And I think it's not appropriated that way because the consumers have a disconnect. Whereas the outdoor industry, there's a straight line, you're camping, you're outside, and if it smells really bad because it's polluted or you can't drink the water out of the stream next to your campsite, you're going to work to change that. And it's education. The outdoor community and the advertisers within the outdoor community do a very good job of educating the public. And more and more outdoor communities are doing better and better. But I'll say, although there's a ton of B Corps in the outdoor community that's growing and that's going into all sorts of other companies in all sorts of areas. And so I think maybe advertising the outdoor community can affect more, getting more people outdoor to young age to respect the environment, to respect what we have. I think that is helpful.", "token_estimate": 545, "speakers": ["John Salzinger"], "start_timestamp": "01:01:31", "end_timestamp": "01:01:31", "podcast_index": 56, "episode_name": "EP 56: John Salzinger | The Advertisers Responsibility | MPOWERD", "slug": "ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "guest_name": "John Salzinger", "guest_title": "Founder of MPOWERD", "categories": [], "short_description": "Toxic consumerism, advertising and the marketer's responsibility", "podcast_length": "1:20:40"}, {"id": "015f941e-e4e0-435a-8a28-453b3ce509ed", "episode_id": "fde769da-fa02-42e1-88b0-86f7ca00aa72", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4863", "chunk_index": 24, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:04:15): Are there any, I don't know, double standards or things that you look at within the outdoor industry that it looks like we're doing good things, but we should be cautious of certain paths? Do you see anything like that\n\nJohn Salzinger (01:04:33): Everywhere and everything? Yes. So I'll give you the example of solar energy. So solar energy is obviously clean, it's abundant. And once you have your panel, if one day we get to the point where fossil fuel companies aren't plugging it into the grid, it would be relatively free based on having that panel and maintenance on it. But you still have to make a solar panel. And a solar panel isn't the best thing for the environment. And so in our case, we're very careful on our supply chain, on how it's made, on how it's transported, make sure that there's zero carbon emissions in that. But that's not easy to do, and it comes at a cost. And so across the entire industry, there's choices to be made. And that's why we're not just a B Corp, but a benefit corporation. A benefit corporation is judged on profit and growth and impact by your shareholders. They can literally sue the company if you don't provide impact like you said you would. And so I think that's a good structure on holding accountability. That might be a good approach to it, in my opinion.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:05:50): Yeah, interesting. What are other certification programs that are out there that help regulate some of this stuff? You've mentioned the Benefit Corporation, B Corp. What else is out there that people can look into if they want to learn more?", "token_estimate": 401, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "John Salzinger"], "start_timestamp": "01:04:15", "end_timestamp": "01:05:50", "podcast_index": 56, "episode_name": "EP 56: John Salzinger | The Advertisers Responsibility | MPOWERD", "slug": "ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "guest_name": "John Salzinger", "guest_title": "Founder of MPOWERD", "categories": [], "short_description": "Toxic consumerism, advertising and the marketer's responsibility", "podcast_length": "1:20:40"}, {"id": "dd289b10-3c2d-420c-b28d-4b214adf9593", "episode_id": "fde769da-fa02-42e1-88b0-86f7ca00aa72", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4863", "chunk_index": 25, "content": "John Salzinger (01:06:07): Yeah, that's a great question. So first of all, there's a lot. There's like gola, which is an organization that certifies works with Lighting Global, the World Bank on certifying solar products in Sub-Saharan Africa and Asia, Southeast Asia. There's a ton. What I would say though is even those organizations get co-opted right now. And so the scary part of all that is you have to do your research and study on who is doing what for whom and why. And some of these standards are done for the right reasons and some simply aren't. Some are more macroeconomic political policy that are hidden behind the scenes, and some are making sure that people get products that work the way they say they are and are not harming the environment. I would answer your question by saying we need more independent organizations that are tied to these huge global infrastructures to do exactly what you're saying. And I think the digital world affords the opportunity for people to do these sort of plugins basically, and provide information. I think that platforms like Reddit, people can really speak truth to power freely are very important. I guess the advertising agencies or the advertising world in the past has been a big part of that, right? So I've mentioned Consumer Reports or Better Business Bureau. These things are dying out. I think in the digital, I think it's a huge opportunity in the digital space for people to start. Companies that do more of this investigative certification and advertising of that is something that I think is missing because of the deregulations on the government side.", "token_estimate": 406, "speakers": ["John Salzinger"], "start_timestamp": "01:06:07", "end_timestamp": "01:06:07", "podcast_index": 56, "episode_name": "EP 56: John Salzinger | The Advertisers Responsibility | MPOWERD", "slug": "ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "guest_name": "John Salzinger", "guest_title": "Founder of MPOWERD", "categories": [], "short_description": "Toxic consumerism, advertising and the marketer's responsibility", "podcast_length": "1:20:40"}, {"id": "0056b500-a3af-4e80-882c-bf0e494b2fdd", "episode_id": "fde769da-fa02-42e1-88b0-86f7ca00aa72", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4863", "chunk_index": 26, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:08:25): Interesting. So we've mentioned, cause marketing. I want to talk about aspirational marketing, which is very common in the outdoor space, and to define it well, you look at an Instagram photo and there's some guy or gal, I don't know, reaching the summit after this big push and it's epic and there's a company selling a jacket and the message is, if you buy this jacket, you two can stand on top of this mountain and champion all of your, I dunno, conquer all of your fears, whatever. When we love that stuff because that's what we, in the outdoor industry love to do. We will love to get out and push ourselves. My question though is in a time when so many people are looking at social media, when depression and mental health issues are on such a rise because we compare ourselves to the world around us and our friends, and now our friends in the world are accessible through a screen we can see anything and compare ourselves to them. What responsibility, if any, do the corporations do? The brands who use aspirational marketing have when considering that their consumer's mental health is at risk. And not saying that that photo of the summit leads to someone being depressed, but I feel like it's a cumulative game and when we're constantly exposed to those giant wins or those successes we won feel inadequate, and then two, we feel like, oh, maybe I need to buy that thing.", "token_estimate": 353, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:08:25", "end_timestamp": "01:08:25", "podcast_index": 56, "episode_name": "EP 56: John Salzinger | The Advertisers Responsibility | MPOWERD", "slug": "ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "guest_name": "John Salzinger", "guest_title": "Founder of MPOWERD", "categories": [], "short_description": "Toxic consumerism, advertising and the marketer's responsibility", "podcast_length": "1:20:40"}, {"id": "839bd666-585c-4b91-a4b9-8d0fb0c259c8", "episode_id": "fde769da-fa02-42e1-88b0-86f7ca00aa72", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4863", "chunk_index": 27, "content": "John Salzinger (01:10:12): Yeah, I think of Nike and I think of Jordan, there was the just do it sort of insinuating that you'd be, you'd have similar skills to Michael Jordan. Then they just straight up said, be like Mike, which you're not going to be sorry. I think aspirational advertising is such that you got to understand it's, you got to take it with a grain of salt. I understand your point. I've always felt that a positive, beautiful image of someone enjoying a product is a great way to get someone's attention. I don't necessarily see that as disingenuous unless you're being literal. I think it's okay to get people to go outside and hike and camp because you show them a beautiful place. And if it's a great company like Cotopaxi who's doing some social good involved in it and the purchasing of those products ends up with Cotopaxi and thus refugees in this case doing better. (01:11:35): I think that's fantastic. I think there's a point where there's a line where you're false advertising, and that's the distinction where I think it gets dangerous and that's more in like pharma, that's more in a car lease or a bank loan or something. That's not quite what it's made out to be. I think that's harmful and hurtful. I think a beautiful aspirational photograph or saying that this sneaker is what Michael Jordan wears. You take it with a grain of salt. Yeah, I don't think that's our biggest issue. I think some positivity these days is fun. If it's taken as such, it's okay. I mean, you're not going to hate on a movie because it was shot in a beautiful place and you're not going to hate your life because you're not in the French Riviera today where the movie was shot. So I don't take that as seriously. I do think there should be a balance though. I think messaging, progressive messaging along with that is helpful.", "token_estimate": 460, "speakers": ["John Salzinger"], "start_timestamp": "01:10:12", "end_timestamp": "01:10:12", "podcast_index": 56, "episode_name": "EP 56: John Salzinger | The Advertisers Responsibility | MPOWERD", "slug": "ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "guest_name": "John Salzinger", "guest_title": "Founder of MPOWERD", "categories": [], "short_description": "Toxic consumerism, advertising and the marketer's responsibility", "podcast_length": "1:20:40"}, {"id": "4d86ddd8-21dc-417a-84a5-90229cfe8a1c", "episode_id": "fde769da-fa02-42e1-88b0-86f7ca00aa72", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4863", "chunk_index": 28, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:12:46): I feel like there are people out there who do feel like they need to be in the French Riviera when they see whatever, and I am not saying that this is advertising's fault. I mean, I would assume that there's also some things that individual can work on from their own character and their values, but I guess again, I'm just curious, what role does all that play?\n\nJohn Salzinger (01:13:11): At the end of the day, it's almost, if you look at it, you can look at it in two ways. You can say, oh, that's messed up. I don't have that dah, dah, dah. Or you can say, oh, somebody has that and I don't, and so there's inequities and that can also lead you to take action. Right, and is it the advertiser or the brand or the access to Everest? I think it's the access to Everest. I think the answer is in the fact that only some people have access to Everest or only some people have access to taking time off of work, whether it's to go somewhere beautiful or to travel somewhere exotic or down as fundamentally as to take the time off on a Tuesday to vote. I think there's just inequities in society and I think what you're talking about is a reflection of that, but cause\n\nCole Heilborn (01:14:09): Interesting. Yeah, I never thought about it that way. I appreciate your positivity. I generally am more of a PEs pessimistic person in life and it's good that I feel like you're kind of balancing my pessimism with your optimism with all this.\n\nJohn Salzinger (01:14:26): Well, then you're in big trouble. I think I'm a,\n\nCole Heilborn (01:14:28): Yeah, it's pretty good. What does give you hope? What gets you excited? Obviously with the work that you guys are doing and empowered, but beyond that, when it comes to just some of these heavy topics that are hard to figure out where they start and where they", "token_estimate": 457, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "John Salzinger"], "start_timestamp": "01:12:46", "end_timestamp": "01:14:28", "podcast_index": 56, "episode_name": "EP 56: John Salzinger | The Advertisers Responsibility | MPOWERD", "slug": "ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "guest_name": "John Salzinger", "guest_title": "Founder of MPOWERD", "categories": [], "short_description": "Toxic consumerism, advertising and the marketer's responsibility", "podcast_length": "1:20:40"}, {"id": "777695f4-5d4b-48be-914a-72b31ac68c08", "episode_id": "fde769da-fa02-42e1-88b0-86f7ca00aa72", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4863", "chunk_index": 29, "content": "John Salzinger (01:14:46): End. Yeah, ad empower. Just to start, we have 14 people that are incredible, so they motivate me. You fill yourself up your company, up the room up with people that are smarter than you. They're driven. They're all there for the mission. It's inspiring when we get stories back on how their lights have saved someone from a calamity or a natural disaster or have helped someone whose death communicate after a blackout and power goes down. You can't sign in the darkness. There's a million areas in empower that empower me, give back to me, why I do it, why we do it. (01:15:44): Especially our CEO SGA Jung, who was from South Korea before it was the tiger economy lived. She was basically who our stakeholders are today and made her way all the way up to ENG and now the CEO of Empower. Yeah, I mean, to me, this is a core question that we all have to really ponder and think about what is going to drive us personally. When I see things change, even the smallest amount, it makes me feel better when I can help someone like this wonderful kid, Quie Jackson, I mentioned him every chance I get, who gives out blessing bags? He has an organization, young Kid, 13, 14 years old. Project I am gives out blessing bags to tens of thousands of homeless people. That motivates me. (01:16:47): What motivates me and gives me hope is the alternative. My son is three and a half and I want make sure there's a better world for him, but when I see his friends or other little kids, I want to make sure that we're leaving a better world for others. So maybe it's less hope and more motivation when problems, I'm a part of the whole and I want to, I represent that for those I leave behind. And yeah, hope is the only way to do it. I mean, when you're down and someone gives you hope, it feels good, so why not reciprocate? Right?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:17:38): Yeah, that's a good point. Instead of looking at these issues from the outside looking in, I think that's a good point to remember is that we are all involved with this stuff. We're all on the inside circle. This isn't someone else's problem or opportunity. We're all in it.\n\nJohn Salzinger (01:18:01): Oh, we're in it. Yeah.", "token_estimate": 550, "speakers": ["John Salzinger", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:14:46", "end_timestamp": "01:18:01", "podcast_index": 56, "episode_name": "EP 56: John Salzinger | The Advertisers Responsibility | MPOWERD", "slug": "ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "guest_name": "John Salzinger", "guest_title": "Founder of MPOWERD", "categories": [], "short_description": "Toxic consumerism, advertising and the marketer's responsibility", "podcast_length": "1:20:40"}, {"id": "021be9bd-8704-4fa3-b63e-f535439350e5", "episode_id": "fde769da-fa02-42e1-88b0-86f7ca00aa72", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4863", "chunk_index": 30, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:18:05): Well, I feel like I could keep asking questions for at least the rest of the day, but I know we're kind of running out of time. John, I want to thank you for chatting and sharing your perspective. If people want to follow along with you or empowered, or if people just want to, I don't know, go somewhere where they feel like they can contribute to some good, where can they go?\n\nJohn Salzinger (01:18:29): Yeah, I mean, I'm on LinkedIn, John Inger, sung Jung is our CEO. She's also on LinkedIn Empowered, M-P-O-W-E-R-D-M, power d.com. You can buy a light for people that need it. You can just buy a light for yourself, which supports our model. There's a lot of opportunities to look at good companies on B Corporation. They have a website. For me, the end of the day, take a little responsibility, care a little more about everyone, not just yourself or those in your pod. Take responsibility, take action. Try not to just focus on changing yourself, but try to help others and you'll find that that in itself will change you. But yeah, please help us on our mission, help others and please let us know what you think about our products and our mission, and share your thoughts on social because we want to hear more about what we're doing from you, the consumer and the user of our products.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:19:58): Wonderful. Well, John, thank you again. I hope you have a great rest of your day, and I'm excited to see what you guys are able to continue to do in 21.\n\nJohn Salzinger (01:20:08): Thank you. We have some surprises coming up, some great products, some solutions, all clean.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:20:14): Awesome.\n\nJohn Salzinger (01:20:15): Beautiful.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:20:16): Well, have a good rest of your day.\n\nJohn Salzinger (01:20:18): Thank you, you too. Thanks so much.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:20:21): Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 516, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "John Salzinger"], "start_timestamp": "01:18:05", "end_timestamp": "01:20:21", "podcast_index": 56, "episode_name": "EP 56: John Salzinger | The Advertisers Responsibility | MPOWERD", "slug": "ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-56-john-salzinger-the-advertisers-responsibility-mpowerd", "guest_name": "John Salzinger", "guest_title": "Founder of MPOWERD", "categories": [], "short_description": "Toxic consumerism, advertising and the marketer's responsibility", "podcast_length": "1:20:40"}, {"id": "2586e8a0-5656-424b-a830-76169a3bac6d", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Diana Ralston (00:04): We are trying to embark on a pretty big project within Navajo Nation and people on our team have great experience building programs and building authentic relationships with corporations or other nonprofits from the ground up. So it's daunting, but it's really exciting to think that within three or four years maybe we could have a huge impact on the food desert and the water crisis in Navajo Nation.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:30): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Holborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Port Side Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo. Today I'm sitting down with Diana Ralston, the founder and executive director of Candidate. Diana. How are you?\n\nDiana Ralston (01:11): I'm great. Thanks so much for having me.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:13): Yeah, thanks for joining us today. I'm super excited to be chatting. It's always fun to chat with some people from the nonprofit space. We definitely have not had as many folks from the nonprofit world on the show, so I'm excited to share this perspective. So quickly before we jump in, I was looking through your guys' website and you guys have a really impressive, just kind of like glimpses to what candidate has done over the last few years. And just to kind of read through it so people understand briefly who you guys are. You guys have raised $6.3 million since 2013, recycled 72 million cans, built 6,610 bikes and skateboards, donated 2,505 instruments and donated 1.9 million cans of water. That's impressive. Thank\n\nDiana Ralston (01:59): You. Yeah, when you read it off like that, it definitely sounds like a lot and it has been a pretty amazing journey these past seven years.", "token_estimate": 545, "speakers": ["Diana Ralston", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:04", "end_timestamp": "01:59", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "ec8cd582-9c1d-4adb-a5a1-bb5f96db99be", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (02:07): Yeah, no kidding. So I'm really curious to learn more about the organization and kind of your role with it, starting it and being the executive director. So can you kind of give us, I guess, the high level view? Obviously, I'm curious to learn more about the name and how that came about. So let's just jump in.\n\nDiana Ralston (02:26): Absolutely. So I'll try to keep it short, although the stories are always a bit convoluted and there's a lot to them, but I would say everybody who was involved in getting can aid started up and running at one point all worked in the craft beer industry and specifically for Oscar Blues Brewery. So I was working for Oscar Blues from Crested Butte at the time and had an idea. All of my previous experience prior to working for Oscar Blues was in the nonprofit segment, and I had an idea to combine what I found to be this creative, passionate culture from the craft beer industry with all the knowledge that I had from working and running other nonprofits. And so I wanted to create something that combined those things and wasn't your typical buttoned up? We do one thing as a nonprofit. I really loved the creativity and the passion and the collaborative spirit in the craft beer industry. (03:32): That was the idea behind it. And then in 2013, there was a horrible flood in Colorado, which is where Oscar Blues was initially founded, and it devastated the hometown of Oscar Blues, which is Lyons Colorado, and that's where I currently live. So all those things came together. I was already in discussions with, Hey, let's start this nonprofit. What is it going to look like? What are our program areas? And then this flood happened in September of 2013. So that was actually the catalyst that really propelled us all into action and got can aid on the map and got us our 5 0 1 C3 status. So the name Canned Aid actually comes from that craft beer industry. And our roots are really firmly embedded in the craft beer industry, although we're totally separate entity now. (04:24): Everything was canned puns. Oscar Blues was the very first brewery to ever can craft beer. So a lot of the marketing behind Oscar Blues and the different beers were all puns around the name the word Can. And so canned aid was the way that we came on to the scene. We actually do can and distribute clean drinking water, as you mentioned, but not only is it just canned aid in terms of the water we're coming in and assisting communities, we're helping kids get instruments in their hands and getting kids off of screens and outdoors. So it was a playful name, but it stuck. So", "token_estimate": 655, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Diana Ralston"], "start_timestamp": "02:07", "end_timestamp": "02:26", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "dd98f4b2-8c91-474f-9bd6-4a35f2b6da80", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (05:00): Yeah. So a few different questions. One, did you first start with distributing cans of water during that flood? Was that kind of where it all started?\n\nDiana Ralston (05:10): We didn't actually give water out in the aftermath of the flood in Lions, but quickly thereafter, we partnered up with Oscar Blues and started distributing canned water to other communities in need around the country.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:22): Okay, gotcha. And so what was your background prior to this? And when you say you were inspired by the craft beer community, what about that? How does that affect what you guys do today?\n\nDiana Ralston (05:34): Sure. My background prior to working for Oscar Blues really was almost entirely nonprofit. So I ran a highly acclaimed juried arts festival. I was the program director for a performing arts center. I helped start a nonprofit from the ground up. I was a founding board member of a children's museum, and I also worked for an organization based in Denver, but it's the Western States Arts Federation and they serve 13 western states. So I had a ton of nonprofit experience and was definitely passionate about doing good and giving back. And then I had a short stint working at Oscar Blues in the craft beer world. And I think I loved how, especially at that time, how collaborative everybody was. And you still see it in this wake of this or in the midst of this pandemic. There's a lot of breweries coming together and collaborating on brews, and then all the proceeds help the restaurant industry or people that are out of work and things like that. So that creative, out of the box thinking and we're all part of this one family and it's supportive, was really what I wanted to do with a new type of nonprofit where it wasn't so by the book buttoned up and really pigeonholed into doing just one thing. And so you see that if people look at our website, we have pretty broad program areas, and that was intentional. We didn't want to be stuck doing just one thing.", "token_estimate": 504, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Diana Ralston"], "start_timestamp": "05:00", "end_timestamp": "05:34", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "28a0e948-ba97-490d-9d73-436386fdbca1", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (07:05): Sure, yeah. Talk about the craft beer community being collaborative. I'm actually wearing a hat that's for a film called Ingham. It's a film that's being produced here in Bellingham locally by one of my buddies, Evan Pollock. And it's all about how our community, our craft beer community has collaborated. And I think it's a pretty common story in a lot of small recreation towns, but we've got, I don't know, 15 to 17 or 18 breweries in the community of like 90,000 people and they just keep popping up everywhere. And so that's a whole nother conversation and an interesting topic to explore, but it's cool to kind of hear a little bit about the roots and I guess the origins of canned aid. You mentioned that the intention was to not be pigeonholed, did at the time where you wanted it to go and all the different fields that you wanted to be active in.", "token_estimate": 220, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:05", "end_timestamp": "07:05", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "119e8a2f-a897-4681-b6ad-b42a7143985b", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Diana Ralston (08:03): I definitely had a good idea, and again, because it started within the walls of Oscar Blues, I was thinking of that particular culture and living in lions and knowing what a big part of the community, Oscar Blues and the music that Oscar Blues used to have at their brew pub right down the street, that sense of community and bringing people together to listen to music, to experience music over a beer, but really just a gathering place. So I knew that music, I wanted that to be part of it and having children of my own and knowing how beneficial access to instruments, music lessons, and just experiencing live music, how enriching that can be in a kid's life. I knew I wanted that to be part of it. And then Oscar Blues, at least the original founder Dale, and a lot of the original people that helped grow that company were all really big into the outdoors and mountain biking. (09:02): And so we have that treads and trails program and beyond it just being a great way to live a healthy, active life, as we've seen increase, I have a 18-year-old and a 15-year-old myself, and with the readily available smartphones and tablets, you just see the increased screen time that kids have. And with that has really come a lot of increased anxiety and depression and lack of getting the daily recommended amount of exercise. So the Treads and Trails program, while it was kind of nicely aligned with the culture at that brewery and what we were passionate about, it also actually was helping solve a problem, which is getting kids off their screens and outside. And so not only do we do bikes, but we also have skateboards that we as a community come together and build and then are donated to underserved children across the country.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:56): Okay. Wow. Yeah. So that kind of leads me to my next question, which is what has the journey looked like over the last seven years from starting it to where you guys are now? You work with people all across the country currently?", "token_estimate": 503, "speakers": ["Diana Ralston", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "08:03", "end_timestamp": "09:56", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "871286bb-9340-4642-b970-94a9e4956555", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Diana Ralston (10:09): We do. We have, actually at the end of 2020, we had finally done a program of some type in all 50 states, which was an exciting little milestone for us. Obviously the last year was something that nobody could necessarily predict. And so our programs definitely adapted quite a bit. And so instead of typically with our tunes program, we would work with touring musicians that were on the road anyway all over the country. And so we would find Title one high needs, lower income music programs at schools across the country and pair the music teacher up with one of our touring bands to get instruments donated and then those bands to go perform for students and provide lessons. Now with the pandemic, everything has gone virtual, but it's still been a really effective and impactful way to positively impact kids that are now more isolated. They're learning from home and having that creative outlet where we're donating instruments and providing music lessons from. Oftentimes a Grammy award-winning musician is teaching these kids over Zoom how to play guitar or the fiddle. And so that's been a really unique way, I guess, to keep our Tunes program rolling even during the pandemic.\n\nCole Heilborn (11:31): Wow. Yeah. So how has the team evolved over the years? I'm assuming it's not just you still. Right.\n\nDiana Ralston (11:40): I was the sole candidate for only just about a little over a year, and then Sarah Levitt joined as the program director and she's still with candidate, so she's been with candidate for six years now and she's now our director of operations and programs, and we actually now have five full-time people on our team. So quite a bit of growth in those seven years, which has allowed us to have our programs spread out across the country.", "token_estimate": 448, "speakers": ["Diana Ralston", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "10:09", "end_timestamp": "11:40", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "7f9dc879-385d-4448-8061-a6d30e494f6b", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (12:09): Yeah, no, that's great. A little extra muscle definitely helps go a long way. So a lot of what you're talking about is the identity. You guys are trying to, you didn't want to be pigeonholed, you wanted to do so many different things. I guess I'm curious, what type of challenges have you guys had from the branding marketing side? I know you guys have been going through a pretty recent kind of reevaluation of the nonprofit and kind of who you guys are. So I guess who have you been historically and how is that changing?\n\nDiana Ralston (12:43): That's a great question. So initially, again, with the roots in the craft beer industry, I think the biggest, it was a blessing and a curse. People knew of Oscar Blues and Dale's Pail Ale, and so they were happy to answer the phone or respond to an email when I was reaching out. But at the same time, they assumed that we were fully funded by Oscar Blues and did not need public funding or donors or grants or donations or financial support. And so that was an initial hurdle, and our very first logo looked like a Dale's Palle can. So that was obviously confusing to the consumer and to any potential donors or partners. So we went through a rebranding exercise and our new logo now we've had it for a couple of years, but our tagline has been fairly consistent. It was people powered do goodery, and we've actually in this rebrand and looking at seven years in, where are we going? I think it's a natural part of the process. Seven years in as a nonprofit, here are normal things that happen. And so we really did a deep dive and rewrote our mission and have just tweaked that tagline slightly to Fueled by People power, but really that is the backbone of what we do is we rally volunteers to help us implement all these programs. So without the support of volunteers, our program certainly wouldn't be in all 50 states.", "token_estimate": 474, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Diana Ralston"], "start_timestamp": "12:09", "end_timestamp": "12:43", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "ff33f8ab-8824-49ba-ac10-3903d4345731", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (14:11): Gotcha. So just for context, I always think it's really interesting to hear how companies and organizations evolve over the years. What was your previous mission statement? What is your mission statement now? Because I think so much of branding is one way that I've heard of branding defined is it's not what you say you are, how are the people describe you, but that comes from knowing who you are in the first place and who you're trying to put out there. So that mission statement is often kind that guiding North Star for folks. I'm curious how that's changed.\n\nDiana Ralston (14:46): The previous mission statement said I'm, I'm not going to get it exactly right, but it was can aid spreads, people powered, do goodery through towns, tunes, treads and trails, and love your mama efforts nationwide. And unless you knew who we were and went to our website and looked at those, clicked on those program areas, nobody knew what towns tunes, treads and trails and love your mama efforts meant. So we were really trying to, again, because we're a different type of nonprofit and we do have those broad program areas, we wanted to explain who we are and what we do, but we didn't want it to be a novel. So it is wordy now, but it says, can Aid is a nationally recognized nonprofit that rallies people from all walks of life to build thriving communities. So again, putting the fact on this fueled by people power, that's at the forefront of what we do. We couldn't do it without the volunteers and the support because they are the ones coming out and helping us build the bicycles, build the skateboards, clean up the waterways, recycle the cans, et cetera. But then a secondary line. So that's the primary is that we rally volunteers to build driving communities. And then the secondary line is we distribute clean drinking water, we provide increased access and opportunities for children to experience music arts in the outdoors, and we protect and preserve the environment.\n\nCole Heilborn (16:14): Gotcha. So when did you go through and revise that mission statement\n\nDiana Ralston (16:18): Last week?\n\nCole Heilborn (16:19): Oh,\n\nDiana Ralston (16:20): Okay. So", "token_estimate": 547, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Diana Ralston"], "start_timestamp": "14:11", "end_timestamp": "16:20", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "59962a88-acaf-4fee-984b-4e64b2149a8e", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (16:21): This is very timely.\n\nDiana Ralston (16:22): This is brand new. We had a retreat last week and all five of us, it's heady work to wordsmith and to really try to get at the heart of what your organization is about. But we had a facilitator via Zoom and spent quite a bit of time on that.\n\nCole Heilborn (16:37): Oh yeah, that's got to be super challenging. How did that process go, I guess were there any going into it, did you set up goals? Did you set up or did you work through exercises to come out on the other end with this polished mission statement?", "token_estimate": 145, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Diana Ralston"], "start_timestamp": "16:21", "end_timestamp": "16:37", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "0c55e74c-8469-49f2-882b-fde63fa8f5c1", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Diana Ralston (16:53): We did. We actually had a former board member who has a ton of experience in this realm with nonprofits and branding, and her name is Stephanie Brady, and she did a pre retreat survey for all of us where there were 10 questions and we could look at the results via Zoom. And we were all, even though there were only five of us, we had little breakout groups during the retreat and we had had feedback and we knew it ourselves. Again, it's one of those things where you've got to do the work, but maybe it's a little hard or maybe you think that you can keep putting it on the back burner. And really we got some feedback from some funders and we were just aware as an organization that, hey, we're growing up a little bit and we need to look at our public persona. And I think because we were in marketing and we worked in that craft beer industry candidate is fun and it's a feel good thing, but by only highlighting that I felt like we were really diminishing the impacts and the importance of the work that we were doing and the fact that our programs are addressing specific problems, like I was talking about screen time with kids or so we wanted to try and strike that balance between the kind of fun, maybe edgy craft beer world and a very serious tug at your heartstrings nonprofit. (18:14): And that was kind of the lens that we were looking at. So it was a long process and we definitely had differing opinions, but we had whiteboards everywhere and we're whittling it down and whittling it down. And again, it's a long mission statement still, but I feel like at least if people were to read those two sentences, they would understand, oh, okay, they do these four things by utilizing volunteers and people power.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:42): Was the goal going into that leadership summit conference, whatever we want to call it, was the goal to come out with the refined mission statement or was that one of multiple objectives?", "token_estimate": 490, "speakers": ["Diana Ralston", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "16:53", "end_timestamp": "18:42", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "2d0ccb24-6ce8-4987-a06b-d375d48f56e3", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Diana Ralston (18:53): That was one of multiple objectives. So that was one four hour morning that we did that, and then we had something that we felt was pretty good, and then we took it home, slept on it, and finally finalized that just on Friday of last week.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:09): Okay, man. I'm curious, what did you learn, well, what did you learn about yourself going through that process? Because I feel like especially as a founder, you created something because you personally were committed and wanted to help see this change in these various areas. But now as the nonprofit grows and there's more people on the team, you're no longer the only voice. You have a country of people who you've helped. What were the challenges personally as you were going through some of those reflection moments in the last week?", "token_estimate": 206, "speakers": ["Diana Ralston", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "18:53", "end_timestamp": "19:09", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "724da455-15f9-4085-a760-6abdee4fe159", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Diana Ralston (19:46): I think part of the challenge was keeping it concise. I know the backstory so intimately because I've lived it and breathed it and it was something that I wanted to have happen, but as you mentioned, it's taken on a life of its own. So it was kind of getting out of the way so that I could hear what the team felt. They're the ones actually going around and implementing the programs and hearing from volunteers or talking with corporate partners. So it was definitely an opportunity for me to listen more and talk less. (20:23): So that was actually a great experience. And because we are such a small team and part of our internal culture is really a authentic last year at our retreat, we went through this exercise where we identified the organization's core values and they were authenticity and balance. And so we really do try to embrace that in all aspects. So making sure that what we were saying felt authentic and good to all of us because again, like I said, we didn't want to run the risk of becoming this super. We're not corporate, we're not buttoned up, we're pretty, we're all of us are pretty casual. We're pretty no bullshit type of people. And so we wanted to make sure that that was coming through, but making us all take ourselves a little bit more seriously. I think you and I spoke again about our bios on the website are true, they're true who we are, they're true to who we are and what we like, but they are much more casual and fun than a typical funder might be looking for. They want to know how many years have you been in the nonprofit realm? What is your specific experience? And we do have that as well. And so making sure that we were telling both sides of that story was part of the process as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:43): Yeah. You mentioned that part of what motivated all of this was that you realized the nonprofit was growing up. I guess why was that important? Where is it going and how does this kind of set the stage for where you'd like to go?", "token_estimate": 503, "speakers": ["Diana Ralston", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "19:46", "end_timestamp": "21:43", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "918a458d-2f20-4e1f-a51e-5f1bdf77e341", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Diana Ralston (21:59): Well, I have a personal mission of making Canata a household name, and that's kind of been mine from the get go where even if you don't know anything about us, but the name, so in my head it's candidate is a household name synonymous with doing good so people know, Hey, this is a vetted nonprofit and I can feel good knowing that my dollar 90 cents on the dollar is going to go directly back into programs at this organization. So that is sort of a personal goal of mine, but the growing up is kind of hard, but it's also exciting. We realize, wow, we have you rattled off those stats from the website and we're also forward looking. We don't really often take time to look back and see what we've accomplished, but we've done a lot and it's exciting and it's humbling and we want to do more. (22:58): All of us are hardwired to do more and dig in, and we don't want to lose the grassroots nature of our approach to things. For example, we're trying to embark on a pretty big project within Navajo Nation and people on our team have great experience building programs and building authentic relationships with corporations or other nonprofits from the ground up. So it's daunting, but it's really exciting to think that within three or four years maybe we could have a huge impact on the food desert and the water crisis in Navajo Nation and not being afraid to try it, Hey, we don't have a specific experience yet in Navajo Nation, but that doesn't mean we're not going to give it our all and go in and try to find leaders in that community and figure out how can we help them address that problem. And again, relying on that people power and the sentiment that we all have the ability and the desire to get back and do good.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:54): So take me back to trying to become a household name. That's a pretty awesome goal. I love that. And as you mentioned, I was trying to think of what are the nonprofits that I can think of that I would label as a household name? And the only one I can come up with right now is Charity Water. I'm curious, obviously as someone you probably could rattle off a whole bunch, what are some that you have your eye on?", "token_estimate": 547, "speakers": ["Diana Ralston", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "21:59", "end_timestamp": "23:54", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "243a155f-2316-495d-83bc-4b4558dcd095", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Diana Ralston (24:22): Well, is funny, it's like people know the Red Cross or people know Boys and Girls clubs across the country, things like that. So I guess the desire to be a household name is really like, wow, if everybody knew candidate and associated with doing good, that's amazing. That means our programs have reached every corner of the US and they've impacted children in positive ways and they've impacted the environment in positive ways. So it's definitely a lofty goal. And we talk about, Hey, how do we actually achieve that? And I think it's really aggressively scaling our programs, but finding partners that are aligned and that have the same type of DNA that we do. So hands on want to really get in there, want to listen to the community. I think listening and responding to community needs is something that we've done all along. So again, with these really broad program areas, if we find out and hear back from leaders of a community that they don't need bikes or skateboards, they need something else to get their kids outside, we're not opposed to saying, great, let's dig in. (25:28): How can we help? How can we figure this out? And I think that that's a unique approach as in terms of a nonprofit, and we have maybe, again, because of the craft beer roots and the makeup of our current board of directors, we're entrepreneurial in spirit. And that is exciting because nothing's ever stagnant. We don't have to say no, as long as there's an identifiable problem and we think we have a solution or an opportunity to partner with other organizations to bring a solution to that community, that's exciting. And that's the way we approach everything.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:02): Yeah, man, I can feel the energy from a few states away. When you look to the future and kind of where you guys are going, how important is really knowing for you personally and for the team and for the future team, knowing who you are on those foundational pillars that you described, your core values, your mission statement and everything else that kind of permeates from those?", "token_estimate": 521, "speakers": ["Diana Ralston", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "24:22", "end_timestamp": "26:02", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "c6da8a5d-e43a-40f0-8011-e60576835bf7", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Diana Ralston (26:32): I think the fear always when things are growing is that you're going to lose the initial culture that people wanted to be part of, that they love the excitement, the passion, the spirit. Like I said, the no nonsense, let's all have an equal role and equal say in this, that's the fear. You get too big and you can't keep those things. We are right now, it's all women led organization. There's five women on the team. We are looking to scale this year with gig workers. So instead of adding full-time staff, we're going to train people that can help implement programs for us and they would be on a per gig basis. And I think that that is, we'll see how it goes. I mean, again, it's a pilot program, but I think by having gig workers that get to experience our culture on a part-time basis, then they go out in the world and they become ambassadors for candid, and they're spreading the gospel. (27:28): Hey, have you heard about candidate? We currently have athletes that are our treads and trails ambassadors, and we have a ton of musicians, as I mentioned, that are tunes ambassadors. And so they're all out there spreading the word and helping turn people on to candid and who we are and what we do. And I think that that's a smart way to grow. I hope at least that's what I'm telling myself that I think having those gig workers and then they get a little taste of our culture and they help bring more people that are aligned people to our world, and then it just grows. And it's not a new concept, but that thought of doing good feels good, and it's creating good in the world, it snowballs. And I think that once people come and experience a candidate event and volunteer, whether it's at a river cleanup or an instrument donation or a skateboard build, they see that it's genuine and we're authentic and we're passionate about it, and it's infectious. So that's the hope.", "token_estimate": 476, "speakers": ["Diana Ralston"], "start_timestamp": "26:32", "end_timestamp": "26:32", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "007e3f86-e1df-41f3-9899-2640915672f9", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (28:36): And I got to imagine everything that you want to accomplish. It's a real challenge to actually obviously to create that sort of change. And it really comes from, I'm assuming, as you said, different grants, different corporate sponsors, business partners who are contributing funding to the mission. How important is the rebrand and everything that you guys have gone through to helping that part of the nonprofit succeed and meet the funding levels that you need to then go off and do goodery?\n\nDiana Ralston (29:10): Yeah, I think it is important, and you don't want to swing too far one direction or the other, but with this proven track record now in seven years of impacts under our Belt, it's time to start telling that story more loudly, I guess, and be unapologetic about it. I think, again, speaking as a woman and with the team of women, we're oftentimes hesitant to sing our accolades and pat ourselves on the back because it might seem too braggadocious or too aggressive, et cetera. And internally, we're really working on that culture and saying, Hey, if you've done something great, you shouldn't be afraid to say it. And you never want to get too big for your britches. You always need to stay humble and grateful. And I absolutely am so grateful for the team that I have right now, and for the work that we're able to do this past year, especially the fact that we could continue our programs and figured out ways to adapt them and that we're fortunate enough to be able to work from home. A lot of people don't have that luxury. So I think staying humble, staying grateful, but also having a really lofty goal kind of keeps us all motivated and excited.\n\nCole Heilborn (30:31): Yeah. So expand on that a little bit. You're saying that historically you guys have had a hard time embracing the good that you've done, and has that created problems when trying to get grants or sponsors because people are like, well, we don't really know you guys, but you guys are like, we've done all this stuff, but we feel weird talking about it.", "token_estimate": 518, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Diana Ralston"], "start_timestamp": "28:36", "end_timestamp": "30:31", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "ed0333cf-3b7f-4ab6-ac76-bcd62a7d4a29", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Diana Ralston (30:49): Yeah, yeah. I think even like we said initially a couple of years ago, if you looked at the website, it really didn't even talk about, Hey, only one in five kids is getting the daily recommended amount of exercise. We didn't identify the problems. It was more like, Hey, this is fun. Come build some bikes. And that has shifted the way we think about the work that we're doing has shifted, and therefore a natural result of that is we talk about it differently. But I think just having the confidence to know that the work you're doing makes a difference and that you feel good about that is maybe new for our organization to feel free to talk about it in those terms. And even, like I mentioned, our bios talking, Sarah Levitt has years of experience working in Haiti, and that doesn't come across in her current bio. So it's like, Hey, not only are there good fun things happening, there are women behind the scenes that have really impressive resumes and experience. And highlighting that a little bit more I think has helped us be positioned or be viewed as more of a serious nonprofit organization by those family foundations and big funders. So I think trying to marry the two and having a good balance between the fun, playful, and the, Hey, we're making serious change and impact in the world is important.\n\nCole Heilborn (32:21): Yeah. Where did that hesitation, do you feel like it came from? Did it come from a certain place? And I guess how have you worked through that?\n\nDiana Ralston (32:30): I think initially it was myself and probably Sarah Levitt who had worked in traditional buttoned up nonprofits. We really felt strongly that we liked this entrepreneurial take some risks and tried to do things differently. We wanted to make sure that stayed intact, and maybe as a result we made things sound more playful and less serious than they actually were. So I think again, seven years of stats and thank you letters from people or just anecdotal stories that have been shared with us as a result of our work made us realize, Hey, we're not actually telling the complete story and we need to start doing that.", "token_estimate": 537, "speakers": ["Diana Ralston", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "30:49", "end_timestamp": "32:30", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "f6f2b2f7-629f-4124-8035-34e6e33e960c", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (33:20): So for folks who are listening who are out there and they've got an idea for a nonprofit, they want to start, I'm sure there's tons of, I mean, a career's worth of advice you could pass along to them, but if you had to narrow it down to something specific as the importance of knowing what your mission is, understanding what your brand is, if you could limit it to that range, I guess anything, what would you pass along?\n\nDiana Ralston (33:51): I think it's important In everything I've done, I always kind of fall back on does it feel authentic to me and am I truly passionate about it? Because you run the risk when you're in the nonprofit world of trying to be all things to all people so that nobody that might potentially fund you or make a donation is offended, and that's just not a way to authentically move forward with your work.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:19): Expand on that a little bit, because part of your brand is also knowing who your audience is. And so have you guys had to, it sounds like at first you were just like, we'll take anyone we can get. And now you've really kind of honed in on who you're there to work with.", "token_estimate": 290, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Diana Ralston"], "start_timestamp": "33:20", "end_timestamp": "34:19", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "bfe6d04b-86b7-473d-8015-0b45917d0954", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Diana Ralston (34:37): I do think that as the type of organization that we are, we're not for every single funder. And that's okay because we want to have partnerships that everybody feels good about and that everyone has skin in the game and has a clear goal. So candidate isn't going to be for every single organization, every single family foundation or funder or donor, but I think not only having relationships with corporate partners or family foundations, et cetera, that really get who we are at our core, that we're hardwired to rally people to help us and to do good, and that there needs to be some trust there and finding funders that will trust just on our track record, but not having had a relationship with us. That's not always easy. Some people really just want to say, I'm going to give to the Red Cross. That's it, period. And that's fine. But we really like starting from the ground up and building these relationships with corporate partners and with foundations, and we have done a fairly good job of it. And I feel like the work that we just did at the retreat where we're refining our mission and our vision can only help. And I feel like the future is pretty bright in terms of attracting new donors and new funders.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:03): So to kind of synthesize, it sounds like for people who are interested in starting a nonprofit, it sounds like really knowing who you are as a founder is probably really important to making sure that what you're doing is authentic and you're able to stick to those core values. But then beyond that, also realizing that you're not there to help everyone, you're there to help maybe a specific few groups of people, and that's okay. Is that what you're saying?", "token_estimate": 432, "speakers": ["Diana Ralston", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "34:37", "end_timestamp": "36:03", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "d6583714-0703-47c5-938a-7e90c4b1dcf7", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Diana Ralston (36:30): Yeah. I think that if somebody was out there wanting to start their own nonprofit, really the advice I would give them is pick one mission and stick to it and a more narrow programmatic offerings that we have. It works for us and we like it, but it has been a stumbling block along the way. People would say, I don't understand what you do. You do this, this, this, and this. It could almost be four separate nonprofits. And again, for me and for the team that we have right now, that works really well, but it's not for everyone. And a singular focus is much easier elevator pitch than trying to explain all of the various things that candidate does.\n\nCole Heilborn (37:11): Why do you think that is? Why are most nonprofits set up in a way where they focus in on one specific thing, just getting musical instruments to kids? Where does that come from?\n\nDiana Ralston (37:22): I don't know. That's an interesting question. I think just historically, that's the way nonprofits have been structured. They do one thing and they focus in on it, and that's all that they do. And so people know, if I want to support cancer research or breast cancer research, I'm going to give to the Susan b Koman Foundation. So I like the flexibility. I like that there are very few things that we can have to say no to because our program areas, environment, arts and music, the outdoors, those are broad and the needs are great, and we have the ability to respond to the needs through these various program areas.\n\nCole Heilborn (38:08): Yeah, no, that's well said. So as you look towards the future, other than if making candidate a household name is kind of the end goal, if there is an end, what are some of the next steps along the way for you?", "token_estimate": 439, "speakers": ["Diana Ralston", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "36:30", "end_timestamp": "38:08", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "41f5a175-321d-46a7-8c9d-7f90dbcc2900", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Diana Ralston (38:23): So beyond just those gig workers that I mentioned, I think a big project that we're hopeful candidate can play a big role in is helping out with what's happening in the Navajo Nation. So again, beyond just shipping truckloads of water as the immediate solution, we're hoping to find on the ground partners and could play a pivotal role in increasing access to clean drinking water there. So to me, that would be a major victory all the way around. Obviously for the people living in Navajo Nation, but in advancing can aid as a more serious, legitimate nonprofit making real change that's on the horizon and what we're focusing on. And then again, with those gig workers, I think the idea and the thought behind it is that we would have gig workers all over the country so that if there's an opportunity to do a program in Seattle, Washington, we've got a gig worker there that's already well-versed, knows the culture, knows what candidate is all about, and can help rally those community members in Seattle to do whatever project we're focusing on there.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:29): Totally, man. Well, I'm excited for you guys. This looks like a lot of fun. And I know you've been doing this for a long time now, but you're only seven years in and I mean, just looking again at your website, look how much awesome stuff you guys have been able to do.\n\nDiana Ralston (39:44): Thank you. Yeah, like I said, it's been a wild ride, but I feel like if we survived last year, 2021 can only be a huge upside for all of us, the whole country, the whole world. So we're definitely excited and we are pushing that aggressive agenda for sure, to become that household name eventually.\n\nCole Heilborn (40:03): No, I love it. And we will definitely be cheering you guys on from over here in the Pacific Northwest. We're running out of time for today, but if people want to follow along with you personally, if they want to get connected with candidate and help out, where can people go if they want to be a gig worker? Where can people go to get connected?", "token_estimate": 517, "speakers": ["Diana Ralston", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "38:23", "end_timestamp": "40:03", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "8fbf866a-b5a9-42c9-be06-ad5956630fa2", "episode_id": "731bebb2-8a22-4f80-b751-5dc8ebfd3702", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4860", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Diana Ralston (40:22): Absolutely. Our website is www.ca DA i.org. They can sign up for our newsletter. They can see by state where programs and projects are happening. So we would love to have everybody come out and experience a candid program. And there isn't yet. There will be a link to apply for to become a gig worker as well there.\n\nCole Heilborn (40:45): Okay, great. Well, this has been super educational. I've enjoyed being able to chat with you and just hear a little bit about how things work in the nonprofit space. And I think really, again, just to summarize how important it is to know who you are and what you're doing and why, and it's fun to hear you guys kind of describing the process that you're going through to figure that out and how that evolves and how that will continue to evolve once you guys do become a household man.\n\nDiana Ralston (41:10): Yeah. Thank you. I appreciate that. Confidence and support.\n\nCole Heilborn (41:15): Absolutely. Well, thanks again and I hope you have a good rest of your day.\n\nDiana Ralston (41:19): Thank you. Take care.\n\nCole Heilborn (41:20): Alright, see you. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 333, "speakers": ["Diana Ralston", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "40:22", "end_timestamp": "41:20", "podcast_index": 57, "episode_name": "EP 57: Diana Ralston | Re-Branding a Non-Profit | Can'd Aid", "slug": "ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-57-diana-ralston-re-branding-a-non-profit-cand-aid", "guest_name": "Diana Ralston", "guest_title": "Founder, Can'd Aid", "categories": [], "short_description": "The messy process of re-branding a non-profit.", "podcast_length": "44:79"}, {"id": "1d967bbe-baad-40e0-a853-7040793a4732", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (00:04): Listen to your audience out there because as great as your product is, there's always got to be something next. We've been around for over a hundred years because we've constantly innovated. We had different materials, lightweight, heavy plastic. It's all just fluctuated based on what our consumer is asking for.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hallborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hallborn. Today I'm sitting down with Alex Sher Beau, the marketing manager at Johnson Outdoors Watercraft Division. Alex, how are you?\n\nAlex Sherbinow (01:18): Hey, cool. I'm doing great. I appreciate you having me on.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:22): Yeah, super excited. We could chat today. I'm glad we were able to stick the landing and get you on the show. It's been a little bit of, I don't know, a few months of back and forth trying to coordinate schedules and whatever else, but we did it.\n\nAlex Sherbinow (01:36): Yeah, sometimes great things are worth waiting for, so I'm glad that we finally got it on the calendar in these crazy times. Sometimes you just got to wait a little bit.", "token_estimate": 420, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:04", "end_timestamp": "01:36", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "03584ff0-872a-4e34-93bd-999414c128ba", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:51): Yep. No, I understand. It's quite a game trying to, I'm looking right now at our guest roster who's being lined up and has been lined up for the show, and it's just so much communication that we're trying to currently figure out ways to make it smoother and improve the process because it's grown so much that now it's sending 10 emails to a guest to coordinate everything is no longer possible just because of the scale of how many people we're trying to line up. But it's just funny how things are changing.\n\nAlex Sherbinow (02:26): Those are good problems to have.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:28): Very much so. Tell us a little bit about your job with Johnson Outdoors. I know you kind of oversee a couple different divisions\n\nAlex Sherbinow (02:36): Of brands. Yeah. I am the marketing manager of Johnson Outdoors Watercraft. I know previously you had some of my colleagues on the camp side from Jet Boyle on the show, so I oversee watercraft. Really what that's made up of is Old Town Canoe and Kayak and Ocean Kayak. We are based in Old Town Maine. That's where all the manufacturing's done. The design team, most of the marketing team. I actually am in Racine, Wisconsin at our Johnson Outdoors Corporate headquarters. So I'm a virtual attendee at most of our meetings, but I guess that works out pretty well.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:22): How is Johnson Outdoor set up? Because you're right earlier. Well, earlier they were I think number like 10 or 15. We had Katie and Sarah from Jet Boyle on the show. And so you guys worked for the same company, but different divisions. How is that structured?", "token_estimate": 404, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Alex Sherbinow"], "start_timestamp": "01:51", "end_timestamp": "03:22", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "cee5b7da-7838-40e2-a724-925c76420993", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (03:38): Yeah, so Johnson Outdoors actually has four different divisions, if you will. Johnson Outdoors is a publicly traded company, and we have four different specialty areas. So there's camping, which is Jet Boyle and Eureka Watercraft, like I said, old Town and Ocean Kayak. We have a fishing division. I mean Coda, hummingbird Cannon, down Riggers, Talon Lake Master Charts. They are kind of the big brand, the big group of brands under the Johnson Outdoors portfolio. But we also have a diving division, and that's largely made up of Scuba Pro. So we have four different areas. A lot of us are in Racine, but we're of course also spread across the country. But it's a really unique collaborative company where we're all in the outdoor space so we can share learnings and even in the old town world, collaborate on some products.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:39): Yeah, that's cool. Do you guys get to collaborate often outside of those divisions?\n\nAlex Sherbinow (04:46): Yeah, yeah. So we actually earlier in 2020 released an old town sportsman line that has seven boats, three different propulsion types. So we have our traditional paddle kayaks, we have pedal kayaks like a bike, and then we have motorized kayaks. And with that, we actually got to work with our counterparts in the fishing division because they make electric trolling motors. So we have Kota powered kayaks now with our autopilot kayaks and 1 0 6 powered by Kota kayaks. So we actually got to take their product and literally design a kayak around it, which is pretty cool.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:26): Okay, well, I'm excited to jump into that a little bit later because I think it's crazy now that we have electric powered kayaks, but to kind of break down the difference between Old Town and Ocean Kayak for us,", "token_estimate": 448, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:38", "end_timestamp": "05:26", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "8b48b5b7-d16e-4f74-8832-3d0fb94652bc", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (05:41): So Old Town was established way back when in 1898. It was founded in Old Town Maine, which is still where we manufacture for both brands today. Old Town was really started by folks who were, believe it or not, kind of floating logs down the rivers. There's a huge lumber industry in Maine, so they cut down and float logs down the rivers, and it's a seasonal business, so people were looking for extra work to do, and the family who started Old Town was actually hiring people and making canoes out of the back of their hardware store, and that eventually turned into Old Town later on. Sam Johnson, who you may know from s SC Johnson, who started Johnson Outdoors and acquired Old Town on the other side of the country in California, ocean Kayak was much more focused on, they actually were the ones who started the sit on top kayak. (06:46): So they joined Johnson Outdoors later down the road, the production facility moved from California to Old Town where it's all based today. So Ocean Kayak is really more of a, if you think of a fun in the sun, something you'd have at the beach, dragging around on the sand, taken out on the ocean, really designed for splashing in the waves. Old Town has a really serious line of fishing kayaks, a lot of canoes, outdoor adventure things that maybe people would view as built in the Midwest, even though it's built in Maine. And that has really expanded out in the last 10 years to go out on Salt Saltwater and do some of the offshore stuff that you'd see today\n\nCole Heilborn (07:33): And your role as marketing manager. Kind of break that down. What does that look like?\n\nAlex Sherbinow (07:37): Yeah, so any given day, I wear a different hat\n\nCole Heilborn (07:42): Currently you're wearing an old town hat. I'm looking at you right now. Old Town hat.", "token_estimate": 456, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "05:41", "end_timestamp": "07:42", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "c0bd9f03-bb61-46a7-9bcc-cae4dace25b5", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (07:48): Yeah. So my job is largely made up of working with a really collaborative team to build bridges between some of the other marketing efforts we have going on. We have a content team, we have a web and e-commerce team, and we have all of our ads and stuff like that. But really what I do is oversee our partnerships, both earned and paid, all of our PR opportunities out there in the world, overseeing some of the photo shoots and a lot of stuff that is more to traditional trade. So when you walk into a store and you see a kayak stand, that's likely something that I was a piece of.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:32): Okay, gotcha. And out of all of those things, is there anything in particular that you enjoy the most?\n\nAlex Sherbinow (08:40): I had like to think I'm a creative spirit, so the photo shoots, the video shoots, making our product come to life. I think that's just incredible. We get to go out, and since I've been here, I've never hired a modeling agency to get someone to go out in a boat who's never been in a boat. It's a lot of people who are staff and friends of the brand and people who have been Lifetime Old Town or Ocean Kayak users who we source to do these photo shoots. So you get to hear their stories a little bit, connect with people outside of the four walls of the office, and I mean, who wouldn't want to spend three or four days on the water versus three or four days at a desk?\n\nCole Heilborn (09:27): You're preaching to the choir right now. I get it. It's interesting. You look at other industries and how looking at the production business, tons of other industries hire actors or higher models for whatever it is that they're doing, but I feel like the outdoor and sports world is one of those industries where you really can't hire an actor unless if they also do the thing that you need them to do. It's just funny talking to some of my other film production buddies. They're going through the casting process and I'm like, we don't cast. We just find people who do it and then they're the people.", "token_estimate": 514, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:48", "end_timestamp": "09:27", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "95d4c9e9-e88d-40e5-bd0b-7831030d7184", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (10:05): Yeah, it is unique. It is one of those things that I think authenticity usually shines through. And perfect example, if you see someone holding a fishing rod upside down, like many people do their first time casting, you're going to get laughed off of social media. So my goal is to keep us away from becoming the next viral mean.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:31): That's a good goal. That's your job summary in one sentence right there.\n\nAlex Sherbinow (10:35): Yeah, exactly.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:38): I'm curious to dive in a little bit more to what you mentioned earlier about the old town, what did you call it? The fishing line?\n\nAlex Sherbinow (10:48): Yeah, the sportsman line.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:50): Sportsman line. That's it. So how did that come to be? How did you guys know that that was something that you even wanted to create? I got to imagine you're sitting around the product dev table and you're like, what do we make? And I feel like that could be a really hard question to answer. Obviously you guys have come out with something pretty phenomenal that still kind of blows my mind in terms of what the technology today can produce, but how did you decide that these are product lines that we want to create and maybe explain what some of those product lines are?", "token_estimate": 324, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "10:05", "end_timestamp": "10:50", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "1719edd1-3cae-4ca2-94d5-804339a6768d", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (11:22): So the old town sportsman line was really born from a couple of different things. One being that we are at our core, the users of our product. There's not a weekend that I get that I'm not on the water doing something, hunting, paddling, fishing, I mean, you name it, that's who we are. So a lot of these crazy ideas come from people in Old Town who are saying, Hey, what if we tried this and we might throw a hundred ideas at the wall and only one sticks. But a lot of that comes from us knowing what products we can build and the things that would make life easier for our own use. The other piece of it is that we can listen and talk with our consumers, especially in the digital world now, to kind of see what they're asking for, what product innovations they would like to see, what makes life on saltwater or what makes life on freshwater better in a river. (12:29): So we went out and designed a full line of sportsman boats and designed it specifically to have a boat for anyone, whether you're throwing a solo canoe on your back and you going to float the river and camp on the banks or get offshore and go five miles on Lake Michigan to catch a salmon. We specifically designed and identified key features that are going to be important to those and shared it across the entire line of boats. So for example, every boat that you can stand in has premium floor decks, so it's comfortable all day multiple rod holders because these are things that you're going to design for fishing, just overall characteristics that are going to be important no matter who you are. And then designed hus for kayaks that are going to be more specific to the water type that you're going into. So seven boats, three ways to travel, one consumer at the end of the day,\n\nCole Heilborn (13:32): And the different propulsion methods that you guys have developed are pretty slick as well. Can you explain those?", "token_estimate": 481, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "11:22", "end_timestamp": "13:32", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "73f05651-ecf0-4b32-a5e3-484e8f8ba68a", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (13:39): Yeah. So of course you always have your traditional paddle kayaks in your paddle solo canoes. What is really unique is our pedal drive system, which if you haven't ever been in a pedal drive kayak, it will change your life instantly because you can go three miles an hour, literally give no effort. You pedal it like a bike, you're sitting high up on a lawn chair style seat, super comfortable, sit on top kayaks, and those kind of allow fishermen to get to a spot and back or someone who's camping and going to the boundary waters cover that ground quicker. And then we have our powered models, which are really what caught the consumer's eye. This year we released an autopilot kayak that actually has a GPS enabled motor in it, so it's controlled by remote control. For those anglers out there who are familiar with iPilot has a full iPilot system. So you can sit back and on your remote control or even Bluetooth on your phone, drive your boat and kind of just hang out. It's lazy man's kayaking, if you will.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:56): You've taken all the work out of it.\n\nAlex Sherbinow (14:59): And the really cool part of that is that we recognize, especially for anglers, that it's incredibly difficult in a kayak to fish a spot or fish a river and imagine a fly angler in a river where there's current pushing 'em around. You only have two seconds to get your fly or your line in that exact spot that you're trying to catch a fish, and that is not efficient for any angler. So with this GPS enabled motor, you can actually spot lock and it will virtually anchor you where you're at. It'll fight the current for you. It'll fight the wind and waves and allow you to be more efficient while you're on the water fishing.\n\nCole Heilborn (15:43): What's the maximum speed? How fast of a current could you be in and it'll hold you?", "token_estimate": 464, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "13:39", "end_timestamp": "15:43", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "e60c6487-22bc-463d-b2b6-8bce3c75d306", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (15:48): Yeah, so it's a 45 pound thrust motor. I take these things four or five miles out on Lake Michigan regularly. I'm going anywhere depending on wind waves current, how much gear I have with me, three and a half to five miles an hour. So definitely safe. You don't want to be going 20 miles an hour in a kayak would probably not be the most stable thing in the world, but it's definitely fast enough to get you there and back quicker than you would probably paddle.\n\nCole Heilborn (16:25): Yeah, that's pretty phenomenal. So transitioning a little bit, I am curious, how are these products received by your consumers? You said going into this that you guys look at it in two ways. You look at it from the perspective of, well, what does the internal team find valuable and where should we put energy into product dev? But also you look at it from the consumer's perspective, obviously, and I'm just curious, what was the response from them and how did you go about collecting that user feedback while you were in product development for these?", "token_estimate": 266, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "15:48", "end_timestamp": "16:25", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "679ddfa6-1dd4-47f7-ac9d-3012f876f1be", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (17:00): Yeah, so because a lot of this technology is largely targeting toward the kayak angler, I mean, like I said, if you're trying to camp, if you're trying to just recreationally paddle, there's a boat in this line for you. But a lot of the technology, the rod holders and accessory tracks are targeted toward the angler. When we launched this, it was at Bassmaster Classic in 2020. The resounding voice of the consumer was what took you so long? They were super excited. We actually won the ICAST best of show category. So we were named the number one innovative product in the fishing industry at the ICAST show this year for our autopilot kayak with the trolling motor. And the consumer, generally speaking, especially the anglers, we're just excited that their voices were heard. Sometimes I think that gets lost in the era we live in where you can communicate with brands, you can send them direct messages and comment on Facebook posts, and you literally are at the fingertips of a brand at any given moment. Feedback has to be accounted for in real time, and you have to be quick to react and agile because if you aren't someone in a competitive world will, (18:30): We had the benefit of working with our counterparts that en coded to speed up timelines a little bit. And our consumer, just like I said, generally, we're super, super excited to have their voices heard and just have a product that's actually tailored to them and their experience.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:51): Do you feel like because there's so much access now between the consumer and the brand, so many ways to connect that sometimes it's actually harder to connect with them and find the product feedback or the user feedback?", "token_estimate": 430, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "17:00", "end_timestamp": "18:51", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "157bba1d-d363-4a8a-b253-ca4553c22ca3", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (19:05): Yeah, it's a great point, and you have to have some measure of scale in your head. If you go into any given Facebook group, there is just a ton of feedback in there. Customer A wants ca, customer B wants seat B, so how do you measure out what's going to be heard and delivered to the consumer? At the end of the day? Not everyone wants autopilot controlled kayak. That's just the reality of the situation. And I think that's where something like the sportsman line really helps. We realize that not any one product is going to be perfect for just every consumer out there, and you have to be able to adapt to specific markets. So yeah, there's a lot of feedback out there and you have to take everything with a grain of salt, but what you'll find is that since they have the ability to be at your fingertips, things will start to, the important things tend to repeat. Multiple consumers will jump on board and say, yeah, that's a great idea, or no, have you tried this? So you sift through it, but it's all important for sure.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:25): And with the sportsman line in particular, how much of the design was taken from feedback from consumers? You've got all this feedback out there, how much of that were you able to actually distill and filter and make it into the final product?", "token_estimate": 330, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "19:05", "end_timestamp": "20:25", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "d000281f-d209-42b7-8041-0162b531f687", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (20:45): Yeah, I don't know that there's an exact number of how many features or benefits we actually were able to take, but what we were able to do, a lot of the HU designs and the shapes of the boat are taken from previous tak models that we had that were successful and that people really enjoyed. (21:08): So we were actually able to go back and look at specific previous kayak designs and take feedback to each individual thing and find a, what's that shared thing that across all models someone's looking for, and B, what are the individual things for each kayak design that we need to change that's going to meet this consumer demand? And I think it's important too that in an industry like kayak fishing, our competitors are also stepping up their game every single day. Technology and kayak fishing is exponentially grown in the last five years, so feedback from five years ago might not be valuable anymore, and you have to be able to weigh out what's important now versus what was important that,\n\nCole Heilborn (21:59): So everything that we're talking about is around this idea of trying to understand your consumer. What do you think understanding your consumer actually means though?", "token_estimate": 304, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "20:45", "end_timestamp": "21:59", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "a42e6b9e-8d10-4bdf-8c10-515abfac18e3", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (22:07): Yeah, there's a lot of things that you can do to understand a consumer. There's raw hard data today. Things like the Facebook Pixel give you direct insights as to what your consumer's engaging with and what your consumers are talking about, what they like, what they don't like. And you can see engagement rates. That's one thing, but I think there is a piece that metrics today just don't measure, and that's how people engage with the brand. And what I mean is the tone of voice in a text message, things can get lost when you jump on a phone call, all of a sudden you're getting a whole new perspective. So we as a brand are going out outside of the traditional social media, outside of looking at Google Analytics and talking with our consumer. And I say that with a grain of salt because we are in a socially distanced world, but we still find ways to engage with people at kayak tournaments where you can socially distance or before COVID at events. There's literally people there that you can have 35, 40 minute conversations with and they feel value in being heard. So I think sometimes we get caught up in technology even, (23:35): And you can take a step back and think about what your grandparents did at trade shows. Trade shows started for a reason. So it's a approach to really understand the consumer.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:49): It's funny you said social media. We are stepping away from the traditional use of social media and going back to the trade show format, which I feel like that's the traditional format and social media is this new thing, and now you're like, I don't even know.", "token_estimate": 407, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "22:07", "end_timestamp": "23:49", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "80c71f66-0b86-4765-bf86-f60cf51d5e86", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (24:06): Yeah. Well, and not to be mistaken, social media is a critical piece of who we are and what we do in marketing, but there are new ways to engage. Great example are these Facebook groups. Anyone who owns an old town Kayak that wants to engage with the community outside of the brand can join a Facebook group tailored to the brand. We don't always run those groups. We might not oversee those groups, but it's a great way for us to see how our consumers are engaging with each other and how we can engage with them as the brand. A lot of times the consumer can talk on behalf of Old Town and we don't have to step in. So it's almost like bringing that traditional trade show mentality to virtual, but at the same time, like I said, going to kayak tournaments and going to events on the water, showing the consumer that you actually care and you're not just a picture on social media and there's a face to this brand. I don't think that'll ever be replaced.\n\nCole Heilborn (25:18): How important do you think that is? I'm imagining you showing up at a trade show, talking to a lifelong consumer, how important is that interaction for that consumer, just for that one off relationship, but also just considering the relationship for the entire brand and consumers as a whole?", "token_estimate": 324, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "24:06", "end_timestamp": "25:18", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "38de5d83-ef63-4221-a103-df8f82567cba", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (25:36): Yeah. Well, for one thing, I think it shows a lot of times your local dealer networks that we're here to support you because our local dealers are extremely important to Old Town, and our sales team in the field does a really great job of supporting them at some of their own events. But at some of these tournaments and smaller trails that we attend, I think it's just really great to be able to connect with someone and have that emotional connection that they might not get with the brand through social media. If you can create one fan and have one positive interaction, sure, one sale may be small in the grand scheme of things, but that fan and that voice of the consumer going out to the world, being that it's a digital world can become massive. Outside of that, it's really cool to hear the stories about people who have grandparents who had old town canoes, and that's the fuel of my fire to keep Old Town around for another a hundred years.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:43): When you look at how brands are collecting consumer feedback, do you see things that, are there new ways that it's being done? Either that you guys are doing it or you see other brands doing it? I guess is there a new way to collect consumer feedback? What does that mean?", "token_estimate": 318, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "25:36", "end_timestamp": "26:43", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "6fff47b4-7682-4d98-9f14-c94576508e66", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (27:08): Yeah, I think there are new ways. I think it's more so than a new way. I think we've just evolved to match the tools that are available to us today. Because we do live in a digital world, it is still important, and most brands still do your traditional market research, sending out surveys, bringing a group of people together to get feedback on a product. That stuff still happens for most brands in our industry, what is kind of new and evolving is the digital means to collect some feedback. And like I said, you can great example in a partnership even you can analyze their audience and see how well they connect with the brand because you can say, okay, we're going to run one ad and see directly how many people from your audience are interested in Old Town. We're able to connect with them in virtual and product showcases if need be. We can send out surveys and engage through social media on a broader scale. We can track and look at what content they engage with, what's important to them. Is it how to content, like how to paddle a kayak versus very technical advanced stuff. I don't know how to do a five day camping trip out of a canoe. (28:40): There's a lot of things that you can measure and kind of design products and shift your focus based on what people engage with every day\n\nCole Heilborn (28:47): And how much of that data on the consumer is very specific. I'm thinking about the typical customer persona. They're 25 years old, they make this much money, they went to this college, and you just go down the list and paint their life versus understanding maybe somewhat on the more emotional side, what are their, their passions, what are their hobbies? What gets them excited about those hobbies? What are their emotional insights? How do you look at those? Do you look at both of those categories?", "token_estimate": 462, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "27:08", "end_timestamp": "28:47", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "6bb4fde6-84fa-4531-b4c4-6d22a7bcb8d8", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (29:23): Yeah, we definitely do. I think it's a lot easier digitally to get the earlier set that you were talking about, the hard data that is demographic information. I live in the Pacific Northwest. I go three days a year. Those things are usually insights that you can pull from a social platform. (29:49): The emotional stuff that I think a lot of brands miss out on are things that are really important to Old Town, so we take time and put effort into, and it's not always saying, Hey, we're going to run this study. It's like I said, going to a kayak tournament trail and having a boat out and saying, 50 people stopped by and we got this suggestion 10 times. I wonder if we should do more research into those pieces. So the emotional stuff might not be hard data that we have in a database or something, but it's stuff that we definitely have a cognizant log of in the back of our head and ongoing conversations about.\n\nCole Heilborn (30:31): Yeah. Do you feel like there's, I am imagining if you're just looking at those two sets of data, you have one, the hard data in the second camp. You've got, let's just say the more well-rounded aspect of a person. If you were looking at the outcome of a product based on the consumer's information you had in camp one and camp two, how would they differ and how would they manifest in terms of either the product or the campaign that you run?", "token_estimate": 352, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "29:23", "end_timestamp": "30:31", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "b3e6c25c-60c2-4e61-bd58-1c6335e1e9ef", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (31:02): Yeah. Well, I, and I hope I'm answering your question saying this. I think that you try to find your happy medium with the data to come up with a line of products and address the wide market that you're looking at. So great example would be if you have someone who's coming to your website and they're 40 years old with two kids at home and a wife in a happy marriage, they might be looking at a canoe because four kayaks can cost a lot of money. So you can shift your mindset a little bit to say, okay, what do we have for families? (31:48): What do we have for people in A, B, C, D? And then dive into the emotional aspect of it. What's their consumer journey look like? Do they have a cabin on a lake? Is transportation an issue? Are they going to have storage If they live in New York City, where are they going to put a canoe? So there's multiple facets of this that make it a long process. It's very well thought out and it takes a lot of conversations, but you have to take the hard data and some of that well-rounded emotional data to kind of create a narrative and really build out a persona of who John Doe is and what product they're going to. Like,\n\nCole Heilborn (32:34): Can you be too biased? So if you're looking at, let's say you see what the consumer wants, you see what you want, and let's say they differ. Do you ever feel like you're maybe too biased or too close to the problem to really see the whole picture? Or how do you balance your own perspective?", "token_estimate": 375, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "31:02", "end_timestamp": "32:34", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "165cbc15-0751-4807-9625-03588042ef93", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (32:51): Yeah, absolutely, and I probably am too close to the product. A lot of times I had be willing to bet that many of us in Old Town get too close to the product. We deal with it every single day. We live and breathe old town. We understand what our products are, what they're great for, and you can miss things because you are too close to it. What we really lean on, again, is our community out there. So if you went on the Old town sportsman owners group on Facebook, we have an entire community of people there who are talking about their old towns. We have people who are making their own products and modifications that they're selling to other users. (33:40): So are those things that we want to entertain are these features that are really important? If someone else is literally building businesses and in their garage every weekend, cutting up great example, there's a guy out there who takes cutting boards and cuts them into the shapes of the product that he wants, the accessory he wants. It's a $5 cutting board from Walmart, and people are asking him to make them for them. So obviously we have a gap there. Is it something we need to address, or is our community already doing that? We also have a really, really good pro staff of anglers and non anglers experts in the field across the country who quite frankly, we can just bluntly ask a lot of questions to and get feedback, and they understand that it's critical to give honest opinions. So being that they're not living and breathing every single day, they might be fishing on the weekends, but they don't think about Old Town 24 7. They can give us honest feedback and know that we're going to take it and they can have their footprint left on our brand.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:51): Sure, sure. Yeah. Are there any misconceptions or lessons you've learned recently around consumer feedback and just everything we've talked about so far?\n\nSpeaker 3 (35:04): Yeah.\n\nAlex Sherbinow (35:10): Thinking on that one, are there any misconceptions about, sorry, you're going to have to repeat the question.", "token_estimate": 522, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "32:51", "end_timestamp": "35:10", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "6fc115cf-e830-4823-96ba-b2d86d1e4e9b", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (35:17): Yeah, no. All good. Yeah. Are there any misconceptions or things that you've learned recently that kind of blew your mind or were like, oh, I never thought of it that way? Around consumer feedback, oftentimes we go into a problem or something we're working on with one perspective, and then we get into it and we leave with a different perspective. Like going through the sportsman line process, what did you learn?\n\nAlex Sherbinow (35:43): Yeah, oddly enough, it came from a video that we watched as a team the other day about doors and how there are bad door designs out there, and no one has ever recreated the door because it gets the job done. (36:00): There are doors out there that look like you're supposed to push 'em and you're supposed to pull 'em and vice versa, and people are running into doors all over the country. That actually got my mind going and made me think a lot about our products, how we market them, how we share, take feedback, and consumer insights. Just because it gets the job done doesn't mean that it's the best possible product. And I think we've done a really good job at Old Town of figuring out what the best possible product is, but there's always room for improvement. (36:37): So I guess the question that comes to mind is how do we take a product that we launched and say, okay, this was really great, people loved it, but we're going to do it better next year or in two years or whenever your next product launches. I think you just need to realize that as proud as you are of an autopilot kayak that won a bunch of awards, there's always one or two things in that product that can be improved. You can spend a hundred thousand dollars on a car and you'll find something that you don't like about it. So imagine what people think about a $2,000 kayak. I think we just need to keep improving and take feedback in stride.\n\nCole Heilborn (37:20): Yeah. Well, and it's probably kind of comforting knowing that you personally and or your team isn't solely responsible for coming up with that in next iteration. As you said, as long as you can just listen to your community, they're probably going to steer you in the right direction.", "token_estimate": 549, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Alex Sherbinow"], "start_timestamp": "35:17", "end_timestamp": "37:20", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "f0720052-03f0-4540-a39e-a1fc44665dd4", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (37:36): Absolutely, and I think that's the, as fun and as launching a product is, I think it's almost more fun. It's see the response to it, take the good, the bad, and the ugly and say, alright, let's do it again, but better. It's a never ending challenge. I don't think there's ever going to be an end of the road, and I had be pretty disappointed if there was, because it'd take a lot of excitement out of the job.\n\nCole Heilborn (38:03): What are you looking forward to next in the next year or two or three?\n\nAlex Sherbinow (38:08): Yeah, I think what I'm really looking forward to is seeing a product that our team worked so hard on come to life in the last year. I mean, the products haven't even been on the market for a year, so some people we're buying 'em in October, got 'em in December, and haven't been on the water yet. So I'm excited in the next year and a half to go out and engage with those people. Hopefully be able to, but even if it's just virtual, I love seeing the stories on Facebook, people sharing pictures of their catches or camping out in the boundary waters or whatever it is. I think that's the moment where you can sit back for a second and realize that you help someone get outside that maybe hasn't done that before, pick up a new passion. That's kind of what I look forward to every day.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:02): Yeah. No, well said. One of my last questions, we're running out of time. Is there anything else that you'd want to leave that audience with just in terms of thoughts or I don't know, anything around consumer feedback and how do you use it most effectively?", "token_estimate": 406, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "37:36", "end_timestamp": "39:02", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "b3e61874-d709-4dc5-af71-3316592df866", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (39:19): Yeah. I would just say this, that most of us in the outdoors industry are passionate about the outdoors industry, and we all know and push for things that we want and expect out of our product. I don't think there's anyone out there who makes a kayak canoe bike camping tent that doesn't think they have the best camping tent, bike, or canoe on the market. If they do, they should find, they should look around. So I'd just say, listen to your audience out there because as great as your product is, there's always got to be something next. We've been around for over a hundred years because we've constantly innovated. We had different materials, lightweight, heavy plastic. It's all just fluctuated based on what our consumer was asking for. You don't stick around for over a hundred years if you don't listen to your consumer, so might as well take advantage of the tools at hand and really dive into it,\n\nCole Heilborn (40:31): And you won't stick around for the next a hundred years if you don't work with your consumer.\n\nAlex Sherbinow (40:35): No, you definitely won't, especially when they're knocking on your Facebook door\n\nCole Heilborn (40:41): Or when they can just go next door and find something pretty similar.\n\nAlex Sherbinow (40:45): Yeah, exactly.\n\nCole Heilborn (40:47): Hop\n\nAlex Sherbinow (40:47): On Amazon.\n\nCole Heilborn (40:49): There you go. Well, Alex, I appreciate you taking the time to chat. If people want to follow along with you personally or with the mini brands that you work with, where can they go?\n\nAlex Sherbinow (40:59): Yeah, old Town canoes.com is a really great starting place for people. If they were to go onto the Old Town canoes website under our Discover tab on the website, there's a whole community page that has links to our Facebook's Instagram owners groups everywhere that you will find Old Town community. That's really what we care about, and obviously, like you've probably heard today, I mean, that's what we pride ourselves on is connecting others on the water.\n\nCole Heilborn (41:31): And if someone listening has an old town or an ocean kayak and wants to give you a piece of feedback, can they find you somewhere?", "token_estimate": 550, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "39:19", "end_timestamp": "41:31", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "6259ed45-fca6-4ea9-b363-1c70145622ec", "episode_id": "bfa36957-9b91-4a94-8732-e624a695a105", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c485f", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Alex Sherbinow (41:39): They definitely can. There's a form on our website or reach out to me on LinkedIn. We take any and all feedback in Stride, and there's a lot of old town canoes out there. I'd be excited to hear anyone's adventures in theirs.\n\nCole Heilborn (41:57): Who knows? Maybe there's the original canoe somewhere out there. Maybe you guys know where it is.\n\nAlex Sherbinow (42:02): We actually do know where it is. We have a record of every canoe and kayak that has ever been made in our factory. Wow. If you have an old canoe, reach out to us if you're interested as to when it was made and who made it.\n\nCole Heilborn (42:16): That's cool. Well, Alex, thanks again and have a good rest of your day.\n\nAlex Sherbinow (42:19): Yeah, you too. Thanks.\n\nCole Heilborn (42:21): See you. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 252, "speakers": ["Alex Sherbinow", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "41:39", "end_timestamp": "42:21", "podcast_index": 59, "episode_name": "EP 59: Alex Sherbinow | Understanding Your Consumer | Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "slug": "ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-59-alex-sherbinow-understanding-your-consumer-old-town-canoe-and-kayak", "guest_name": "Alex Sherbinow", "guest_title": "Old Town Canoe and Kayak", "categories": [], "short_description": "A business doesn't last for 100 years without listening to its consumers. Alex Sherbinow from Old Town Canoe and Kayak joins me on this episode to discuss how crucial it is to listen and understand your consumer.", "podcast_length": "44:81"}, {"id": "ff668f34-6cf7-4cb8-ba4e-a7d71f54cfbb", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Mike Somerby (00:03): I mean, that's something I feel lucky as a marketer to have at my disposal. It's not like what do we think of next? Oh, we're a little content poor right now. Let's just go to the ranch and see what they're doing because it's going to be interesting and it's going to be unique and it's going to be real.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:21): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hibour, and today I'm sitting down with Mike Summer be the director of marketing at Duckworth. Mike, how are you,\n\nMike Somerby (01:00): Cole? I'm doing fantastic. How about yourself?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:03): Doing great. Yeah, thanks for joining us. You're over in Bozeman, correct?\n\nMike Somerby (01:07): Yep, here in Bozeman, Montana. Taking a call out of my guest room here in my little home office. So excited to chat more about Duckworth.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:17): Let's just jump in, kind of fill us in a little bit about the history of the company and what's your relationship with it?", "token_estimate": 393, "speakers": ["Mike Somerby", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:03", "end_timestamp": "01:17", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "dd3c1c66-fff5-4dcb-99ac-28aee7876667", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Mike Somerby (01:24): Yeah, so I've been the director of marketing now for about eight months here. I started basically the start of July, 2020. Before that though, just a little bit of history, the brand is about seven years old. It was founded by a fourth generation ranching family here in Montana. They're based out of Dillon, which is a little west of us, southwest of us here in Bozeman. Fast forward to 2020 where I come into play, the company basically underwent a massive business model shift with COVID coming out of left field. Obviously for all of us, it was critical for us to gain a little bit more flexibility in how we sold our products and switching to a direct to consumer model was pretty much essential. Obviously, I think a lot of brands have done pretty well despite maybe maintaining a mostly wholesale picture for their brand. However, we just decided that this is something we've been wanting to do, take a silver lining out of the downturn that we saw there early on with COVID and do a complete 180 and go direct to consumer. So myself and some new teammates within the Duckworth crew were basically brought in to make that transition possible, and it's been a really fun ride because it feels like starting a company all over again from scratch, and it's going really well. So we're thrilled and we're excited that we were able to take this opportunity and run with it.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:01): It's interesting chatting with folks on this podcast. I don't know what number Duckworth is, but you're one of quite a few who have used COVID as an excuse or maybe just motivation to go the direct to consumer route. Recently we had Rich from Obos on the show and they were doing the exact same thing actually from your neck of the woods.\n\nMike Somerby (03:24): Yeah, it's fellow Bozeman brand.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:26): Yeah. So it is interesting just to kind of see how people are responding and I mean, yeah, way to look at it as a silver lining, I suppose.", "token_estimate": 495, "speakers": ["Mike Somerby", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:24", "end_timestamp": "03:26", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "d04fedd4-bc31-4bea-9d20-1654fe81d8c8", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Mike Somerby (03:35): I mean, I think that there was really two ways you could look at the situation, which was to either roll over or adapt. And obviously with a brand like ours and with the players involved, there's a lot of grittiness that's been built into the DNA of this brand. I mean, it started with a ranching family here in Montana across four generations. They've probably seen some pretty times where you needed to roll up your sleeves, and this is just one of those instances. So I think it's definitely part of our brand too, to try to adapt and persevere, and I'm really glad to hear other brands were able to make the same decision because I know a lot of brands and are still suffering or are gone altogether. So yeah, it was really important to us and I'm glad we again took that opportunity.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:30): So tell me a little bit more about the history of the company, because I think you guys are incredibly unique in that you were started from this fourth generation ranching family, and you still work with the descendants of that family on the ranch to raise sheep and create some pretty amazing products. Kind of fill us in on some of the details,\n\nMike Somerby (04:52): And I think it's actually worth clarifying too, or putting this in a little context. So whenever I get calls from any company trying to work with Duckworth, do an integration with their website, provide a service if they're not from Montana, it's almost like a hundred percent consistency that they're going to ask. Do we watch the show Yellowstone? I don't know if you've seen the show Yellowstone?\n\nCole Heilborn (05:14): I have seen it, yes.", "token_estimate": 413, "speakers": ["Mike Somerby", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:35", "end_timestamp": "05:14", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "20289625-4381-41f6-8d2b-fe4e76ce89c6", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Mike Somerby (05:16): It's pretty funny, and this is probably what I was doing to people from Texas 15 years ago asking, did they watch Friday Night Lights? They might or they might not, right? I mean, it's just kind of a show on, but it's funny, a ranching operation in that show, and I'm just trying to paint a picture for the audience and the ranching family in that show is basically kind of overseers in a way that they're not really part of the work. I just want to clarify that our ranchers are, the individuals who started this brand are literally the same people who are day-to-day working the lands, working the sheep, maintaining quality control at that very elemental piece of the brand. And so they're super hands-on from across the board here, and I just wanted to make mention of that, but a little history about the brand is that a lot of wool purveyors in the United States are subject to the commodities market. (06:17): And one of our founders, John Heli, who's sort of the patriarch of the heli family right now, and he basically decided that to hell with waiting on the commodities market to be opportune for him and his family to grow and sell wool. So he basically formulated with the help of some partners and a way to control that process and take his own wool, which by the way is his life's work has been making his family's wool genetically off the charts. I mean, the wool itself is just incredible quality. It's got a tremendous amount of crimp and loft. I mean, these sheep have to survive with that fleece from in temperatures ranging from 90 degrees in the summer to minus 40 in the winter. So you can imagine what the conditions require of those animals, and then that same fiber is put into our products, obviously. (07:22): But anyway, so he worked on the genetics and he worked on this business idea and it basically was the perfect fusion of those two ideas of his to start a brand like Duckworth where he has a way to showcase this incredible American wool while also accounting for the fact that the commodities market doesn't always play in favor of the hardworking individuals that are going out every single morning and coming in late every single night to make that product possible. So it's a really amazing brand from that specific standpoint to compound on that. I think a lot of competitors within the space, a lot of competitors within the world of wool, they have sources for their wool that come from 10 different countries in three different continents, and all of our wool comes right from our ranch. I mean, what you see is what you get, and that is what makes us so special and has created such a fan following. And then to compound on that even further, we have a hundred percent American made supply chain where we oversee every step of the process. So we're not just saying, Hey, take this wall. We'll see you in a couple months. Hopefully it all works out. I mean, we're on a day-to-day call with every person at every stage of this process to make sure that we're getting the quality and the consistency and just the performance that again, is growing us into a fan favorite for a lot of people within the outdoor space.", "token_estimate": 795, "speakers": ["Mike Somerby"], "start_timestamp": "05:16", "end_timestamp": "05:16", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "6363ba37-bc41-45c3-99f6-5c6c105cfd54", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (09:00): So when you say that the ranchers are integrated into the company, does that mean they're on payroll type of thing?\n\nMike Somerby (09:08): They're definitely benefiting from the growth of Duckworth, and specifically, I don't work day-to-day with John, who again is, he heads up the ranch, he's our head rancher. I work day-to-day with one of his sons, Evan Helly, who is our production specialist, and he productions manager, and he actually takes that raw wool and sees it through to garment. So that's who I'm working with really intimately.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:42): Okay, gotcha. So I guess to kind of dive a little deeper into, I think what makes a Duckworth unique is some of the stories that you guys have told on your website that I think paint a really nice picture around explaining how you work with the ranch and how the ranch works with you. And two of those maybe you could elaborate on are your shear and shred and the sheep trail. Can you kind of paint us a picture of what those are? So I think our audience can better just understand just I think the intensity of care and focus that you guys put into the ranch.", "token_estimate": 288, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Somerby"], "start_timestamp": "09:00", "end_timestamp": "09:42", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "ea50fe27-2aa0-413d-b519-1cf64b99f98a", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Mike Somerby (10:21): Yeah, so I think it's a really good place to start by saying that I feel really lucky as a marketer to not have to fudge the truth all that often at all really. I mean, it's something that I just don't have to think about. And I've worked in jobs as a marketer where you're really trying to put a spin on an idea or a product to entice someone to think it's something more than it is or something completely different in some instances. So I feel really fortunate that that's not a part of my day to day. I come from Connecticut, and that would be really hard to sell someone and get someone excited about something from Connecticut, for something from Montana. That brand in and of itself is really potent right now. We're getting a huge influx of people trying to move here. It's really hot. (11:17): And that concept, Montana is an idea that I think the world over is really resonating with people for the qualities of space and perceptions of freedoms and limitlessness and all of these facets that make it such a potent idea. So those two stories, sheer and shred and sheep trail are made all the more special because of that fact. Because you can really, as a consumer, as someone who's reading these witnessing scenes from these stories, put yourselves in this almost total other universe. It's something unlike you've ever seen. The first time I went on Sheep Trail, which I think of the two is it's one of the most amazing outdoor experiences I've had in my entire life. We work two times a year, sheep Trail, one in the summer and one in the early fall. And basically we operate on a mixture of public and private lands and we bring these sheep six days through the mountains to bring them to summer grazing pastures.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:32): So just to be clear, how many sheep are we talking about when you're saying you're going along with them on this trail?", "token_estimate": 474, "speakers": ["Mike Somerby", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "10:21", "end_timestamp": "12:32", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "37ca58f6-3c1d-47fd-aea1-12a836195746", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Mike Somerby (12:38): Yeah, it's around 10,000 sheep and it's pretty impressive to see an operation of this scale moving through the mountains. And there's so many elements to the story that it's almost like out of a movie. So we have sheep herders who live with the sheep almost 365 days a year, and they're out there in the field with these animals, protecting them, making sure they're getting water, making sure they're getting plenty of food, making sure the landscape is protected. We can't have sheep running through a creek bed or what have you. And we got to make sure that we're respecting the lands on which we rely obviously. But also on top of that, there's guard dogs, 125 to 150 pound dogs that are there to protect them. They're sheep dogs that are highly intelligent, that are basically the right arm of these sheep herders and the ranchers. (13:39): But yeah, it's about six days in which at least in the summertime, these sheep are moving up and over the mountains and going through some of the most amazing back country wildflower fields, mountaintops almost. It's funny because being out there with the ranchers, this is something that they've seen their whole lives. This is something they know really intimately. And for me, I mean my jaw must've been dragging on the trail the entire time. I was just like, I couldn't believe what I was seeing. It was something borderline spiritual in a way. And I actually asked the ranchers, or at least Evan and humans have had a relationship, an agricultural relationship with sheep for, I mean thousands and thousands of years now. And this is something that you can find in some of the most ancient stories on paper, on stone, what have you. And I asked them, is this something that you feel like you're sort of carrying a torch? (14:43): He said, absolutely. So it's a really incredible moment, and we do like to think of it as the last odyssey of North American agriculture. When you're working six days in a row, 50 lateral miles, thousands of vertical feet with that many animals, it's a pretty intense voyage to be sure, and a lot can go wrong, and it's definitely because of the expertise of those individuals that nothing does. I'm talking things go wrong, like sheep could go off a cliff type of thing or I could, the marketer could. So that's something that I think that when you can capture that story and when you can paint a picture for people, for someone like me growing up in Connecticut, that is just as a consumer, something you want to see out of your product, I think these days it's not necessarily enough just to have a killer product. (15:48): People want to know what's under the hood, and people want to know what you're all about, who the players are, what you stand for, what you believe in, and the level of this animal husbandry, this stewardship for the land and this just epic Montana moment that is sort of frozen in time are things that people really resonate with. And that's why Sheep Trail is such a potent piece of marketing and storytelling for us, and it's something that we can rely on twice a year to happen. It's got to happen. It's essential to the shirt that you end up wearing. Without it, the sheep don't get enough of what they need to produce that wool, and they actually don't experience the conditions they need to also produce that fine of a wool. So without it, there's no duckworth. So like I said, there's no BSing, there's no fudging. (16:44): This is a real thing that happens and it has to happen, and I feel really lucky to be able there to be there and capture it and try to then portray that to our customers and potential first time buyers. That other story I mentioned, which is shear and shred just as essential, it's not as sexy because it takes a lot of hard work to shear 10,000 sheep of their fleece. As you can probably imagine, it's giving a multi hundred pound animal a haircut on 10,000 times over, but all the same. I mean, that's where a lot of the magic happens too, because that's where our ranchers and other partners of the business take literally every single fleece and then grade it for quality. And that quality ends up ultimately defining what that fiber will be used for. We don't put our low quality or rather more coarse fibers say in a t-shirt or in your base layers because when you put it on your skin, we want it to be soft, we want it to be comfortable. (18:01): A lot of people in popular culture have built up this idea that wool is always inherently really itchy and really uncomfortable. I think grandma's knitted sweater at Christmas time or whatnot, and that's something that we want to avoid and that's a stigma we want to break down. And that's where a really careful scientific process happens. These fibers are analyzed under a microscope, though. It is said that John Helly, that founder and head rancher can apparently pick up pieces of wool and tell with his fingers the quality. Yeah, so it's funny. Someone referred to him recently as the Mick Jagger of wool, which I think is pretty awesome, but he's got it, and it's something that he just knows the back of his hand. So yeah, when he picks up that piece of wool, he can tell you, but we have computers there just to double check, and those computers will dictate our entire product line in a sense, because it tells us how much we have to make X and how much we have to make this and that. (19:09): And that's a really cool piece of it too, is we are not going to force a product to happen. This is spun gold. It's a finite quality, it's rare. You're not going to find this everywhere. And there's certain products of ours that are just by nature of the beast and by nature of how wool grows are always going to be a commodity because there's just simply never enough wool to keep up with the demand. So it's really exciting to actually have a couple products like that that are always going to have this very rare nature to them as a result of that wool production. But anyway, yeah, shear and shred is a great time. It's kind of like a party and a celebration in a lot of ways too. We share the sheep, we have some food, we break some bread, share some drinks and stories, and then the next day we go over to a local ski hill there in Dillon. I'm talking one chairlift, two seats per chair and go shred. So it's a blast through and through, and it's the yin and yang of those two stories and the way that they interface with each other that somewhere in the middle Duckworth is forged. And that's where we can hang up our hat and say, job well done, 365 days of the year and it never stops, picks up the next day after both of those events.", "token_estimate": 1666, "speakers": ["Mike Somerby"], "start_timestamp": "12:38", "end_timestamp": "12:38", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "ae5d722d-81c8-40f6-a469-8aa1d2044378", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (20:43): Man, talk about a gold mine for authenticity. That's as good as it gets.\n\nMike Somerby (20:48): Yeah, it really is. And like I said, that's something I feel lucky as a marketer to have at my disposal. It's not like, what do we think of next? Oh, we're a little content poor right now. Let's just go to the ranch and see what they're doing because it's going to be interesting and it's going to be unique and it's going to be real\n\nCole Heilborn (21:09): Kind of off topic, but I'm just curious, how many other brands have their own source, their own material from the same state that they operate out of?", "token_estimate": 157, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Somerby"], "start_timestamp": "20:43", "end_timestamp": "21:09", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "37de7a72-452f-4fde-ae5e-72c4fd12e8ef", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Mike Somerby (21:25): I can't think of any. And definitely the biggest players in the wool space. It's a new thing. And actually kudos to every brand getting involved or at least taking this stand, but transparency is something that's going to be here to stay, especially in garment making with a customer base as environmentally and eco minded as the outdoor space has, you can't really get away with behind the scenes doing something really bad for the planet. And so a lot of companies have actually opened up their doors and said, this is what's going on. This is where we source our wool. This is where we clean it, this is where we spin it. (22:14): And you'll find that those do for every piece of good that does, showing people that here's how we do it, it does really make you think because there's a laundry list of countries and beyond bringing technical know-how and knowledge and a piece of our American heritage back, the wool industry was basically on the ropes and at least in this country, and Duckworth really thought that that was behind some of the ethos of the brand is that's a real shame. I mean, so we're really working hard to bring some of that technical knowledge back to the states because it is, again, it's a big piece of Montana's heritage, but it's also a piece of this nation's heritage. But I like to think of it just as importantly as when you do have that many countries involved, and I'm sure there are good people behind every single one of those stories as well, but when you do have that many countries involved, what ends up happening is you're adding about 10,000 or so miles worth of emissions to the supply chain per garment. And so that really adds up. And from a sustainability side of the equation, I think being so local while yet still trying to grow so big is something that is really important to me. And I personally probably would've had a hard time working for a company in this space that didn't practice that.", "token_estimate": 492, "speakers": ["Mike Somerby"], "start_timestamp": "21:25", "end_timestamp": "21:25", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "9b7db639-c923-4ea4-9201-db4ccf15a1c9", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (23:52): So I know when we chatted previously on our introduction call, you were brought in pretty recently in the last less than a year or so to help with a number of different things. One of which you talked about is kind of the direct to consumer route, but also just being able to share this story of Duckworth with the world. And I think you'd mentioned that that's one thing that historically Duckworth is challenged with is telling that story and getting your name out there and communicating why, communicating the why behind what you guys do. I guess, how are you going about that? And I know part of this equation is also trying to build this base of loyal customers, which is something that it sounds like you've had a pretty niche, really hardcore kind of loyalty group, but you're trying to expand that as well as you expand this storytelling.", "token_estimate": 218, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "23:52", "end_timestamp": "23:52", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "c1fe443d-9d78-4e14-a3c7-2a6d166645ba", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Mike Somerby (24:46): Yeah, I think we have sort of a chip on our shoulder in the office because we do know that we have this incredible product and we do have this incredible story. And I think if more people were able to feel one of our items on their skin as they're in the back country or even just walking the dog and then hear a piece of that story to compliment it, I think that there's a natural magnetism to that combo. And like I said, we're aggressive right now in trying to get both of those out there. In the past, Duckworth worked with marketing partners who in many instances had way too much on their plate. We weren't their sole focus, so they'd come for the day or they'd come for a few hours and they'd do some B-roll pickup shots and they would jot a couple notes and that would be that, and then they'd go spin something up and probably the next day do the same thing for another client. (25:52): And that's been sort of the model for a long time. Actually, I've worked on the agency and production side in a major market, and I do know that that's just the nature of the business is pick up as much as you can. But when I completed Sheep Trail this summer in July, my first sheep trail, Evan remarked Evan Helly that is, he's like, I think this is the first time we've had anyone who's doing any marketing for us actually complete this journey, actually get to the top of these mountains and get back down the other side. And honestly, I almost, I could have collapsed right there so I can understand how it was a bit of a undertaking 16 hours on my feet at one day, 16 hours worth of hiking. So that was a lot. So I get why that can be difficult, but we're on a mission to, even though we're working from the office in Bozeman to eat, sleep, and breathe this brand and put ourselves as close as possible to understanding every single inch of this process. (27:02): So getting the story out there has meant hiring on a really crack media team to on the fly, do some really incredible stuff and be really flexible to us. It's involved really holistically looking at the way we do our advertisements, our media buys, our social pushes, what have you, and see what sort of pieces are resonating with each individual. And like you mentioned, we did have this really super niche, super hardcore group of fans and that's great. And though we did need to expand and we are actively looking to, and it's really just trying to tell the pieces of the story that can bring people together versus put distance between two individuals. Since I've moved to Montana, I think it's really interesting that no matter what side of the political spectrum you're on, for example, everyone cares about the landscape here. There's individuals on both sides who are making money exploiting certain pieces of the landscape, and that's present every state, every country, and it's too bad. (28:21): But they're someone who, again, they're both looking at this place for its natural wonder and people are inspired by it every single day. So getting at the meat of that basic inspiration of why do we get outdoors? Why do we want to spend time in the trees? Whether it's for hunting a bull elk that you plan to harvest and eat for the year, or just simply to go take a walk with the dog. I mean, I think that those two parties can overlap. And we did have sort of a following in this more hardcore Montana based market, but we're finding that quality product is quality product. And if you can get in front of the right people with the same interest, general basic urge to get outside and participate and be well protected while they're out there, you're going to be able to build a fan base that is diverse and varied and it doesn't matter what they, their activity of choice in the wilderness is, or the back country, they, they're going to find common ground over just simple quality. And I mean realistically, what you want to have at the end of the day is someone not thinking about their duckworth when they're out skiing or ice fishing or whatever you want it to be just in the background so that they can just get out and really be thoughtlessly getting into the flow.", "token_estimate": 1043, "speakers": ["Mike Somerby"], "start_timestamp": "24:46", "end_timestamp": "24:46", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "59ba5aef-3322-4fab-86d3-f1c8357d0624", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (29:52): So as you're considering trying to expand the Duckworth story and find, build this fan base, when you look at the sales side of it and you look at the benefit of a loyal customer, are there any metrics out there that you kind of remind yourself of as you're working towards this that help continue that validate that all this work is worth it? And I know there's a lot of ways to quantify is it worth it, but in just the sales way, is there anything that you look at?", "token_estimate": 123, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "29:52", "end_timestamp": "29:52", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "e3b0d061-c6ca-43fb-8530-6f510950b6b8", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Mike Somerby (30:26): I mean, I think one of the things that I want to see when I'm in Bozeman, for example, walking down the street, is I want to see people wearing Duckworth. Bozeman in and of itself is a pretty varied place. I mean, there is kind of all walks of the Montana lifestyle in one spot. So I want to be able to see that people are aware of Duckworth, I want to see that people are wearing it. That's a really tangible, easy way for me to be like, it's gaining traction. We have this quality product, and seeing it just out and about on the street is important to me. We do have KPIs that we look at. Obviously customer lifetime value is one of those KPIs, but it's really at a point where we can definitely feel attention on our inventory, if you will. You can sort of feel this demand. (31:25): We introduce a color or we bring something back in stock and it just flies. We have projections for every single sku, how many we're supposed to be selling per quarter to basically realistically get within some of our sales goals. And when we see a new product just taking flight, that's definitely a good indication that things are really starting to take hold for a really wide swath of individuals. And then similarly Shopify, which is the platform we use. That's great. We can definitely see that. And similarly on Instagram, you can see in your analytics, you can see certain cities growing. You can see various geo geolocations getting a bit larger in terms of reaching a critical mass. And it used to be when I started that it was Hyper Rocky Mountain Hyper West. On both of those accounts, our Shopify and our Instagram. And what I can see is that larger metropolitan and micropolitan areas around the country are really starting to get behind some of the ethos. (32:37): And I think again, that's part of the storytelling. There's just something for everyone. I mean, if you're in Montana, if you're in Wyoming, Colorado, Utah, Nebraska, what have you, any of the Rocky Mountain Saints, I don't know how much Nebraska really is, but you can kind of find a relatability though when you're in Connecticut or you're in Arizona or you're in, I guess Arizona is mountainous, so that's a bad example, but you get what I'm saying. If you're in sort of a market that isn't necessarily one that's traditionally been for us, it's something where people look at and they're like, wow, that looks like a really cool piece of brand identity I want to get involved with. So you can definitely see based on those location metrics that there's definitely a growing desire. And I mean a lot of places, just as we've seen this past winter on the east, get pummeled with snow and cold weather too. So you don't need to be out skiing or facing Montana Sub-Zero weather to need something quality on you, and that's starting to really take shape.", "token_estimate": 711, "speakers": ["Mike Somerby"], "start_timestamp": "30:26", "end_timestamp": "30:26", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "04426993-46b8-4d7c-bfa7-14a5349252ec", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (33:51): How would you define a loyal customer? When has a customer reached loyalty status?\n\nMike Somerby (33:59): I think for us, loyalty can be defined in two ways. If you were looking at it from a very black and white budget sheet sort of direction, you could say that probably once someone makes three purchases, that's just about when you could really say, this is a very loyal customer, but I don't want to steer anyone wrong because I think that you could buy Duckworth once in your whole life and if you love it and if you wear it every day, and if you're telling folks about how much this product has really saved your skin in various situations, that's a loyal product or a loyal customer to me, and that's just as valuable. Someone again, could have just bought a t-shirt and a hat, but if they're on Instagram and they're sharing us to their story or they're sharing our posts to their friends, to me that's a loyal customer that's really hard to track. (35:00): And probably my finance manager wouldn't like me hearing that or wouldn't like to hear me saying that that is what a loyal customer is, though I think he would understand that that in the same way is just as important as someone who's going to make a bunch of buys. Our product too is very durable. So a lot of instances it will take a while to wear out, and so if you're buying once and you get everything that you need, it can last you a long, long time. On that note, just a little tangent, a piece of synthetic, a piece of plastic, you bend it back and forth about 3000 times, it snaps in half. If you had a fiber of synthetic material, if you did the same thing with a fiber of our wool, it would take about 20,000 iterations to snap in half. So it's more than six times as durable from that standing. (35:54): So again, it's kind of funny, I was talking to a buddy the other day who is a photographer and he does a lot of what he calls torture testing products for various companies. He worked with a brand here in Bozeman that he wore their pants for apparently 470 days in a row. He acknowledged there's brands out there where durability is part of everything. And so I just bought a pair of Carhartts because I want to have pants that are going to last me a long time. So if you buy Duckworth one time and it lasts you many, many, many seasons, but you're out there just telling folks at the ski lounge or just in your own neighborhood or even people in your own home that this stuff is awesome, that's a loyal customer to me. And that's really a credit to, in a minor way our storytelling, but in a major way the quality of our wool and the quality of our production that you just can't find anywhere else", "token_estimate": 676, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Somerby"], "start_timestamp": "33:51", "end_timestamp": "33:59", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "b0e73dbe-bb68-4bd9-a426-48ff11cf06c2", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (36:56): To make your finance manager happy. I was flipping through a book that I read a while ago, and I was flipping through it in advance for this because in the book talked a lot about loyalty and brand loyalty. And I'll read two stats that I thought were interesting. One was the most loyal 50% of customers would pay a 25% premium before switching brands. And secondly, loyal customers spend approximately 33% more than new customers do, which I just think is pretty remarkable.", "token_estimate": 125, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "36:56", "end_timestamp": "36:56", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "ef457e35-68f9-44bf-9690-f50b91959068", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Mike Somerby (37:29): And definitely we do. I mean, as a marketer, I acknowledge those hard facts. I acknowledge the data. If you're a VIP, if you're a great customer of ours, you're going to be getting exclusive deals, you're going to get early access, you're going to get all of the things that come with being that loyal customer that has contributed from a data perspective to our bottom line in a meaningful way. And we like to, obviously this is a family operation. This is a really down to earth operation. This is an operation based in a state where people are still very friendly to strangers in the street. And if they do see a stranger, they might try to make that a friend with the right circumstances. I used to live in New York City, are you going to talk to someone on the subway? Probably not. (38:22): And where I'm getting at that it's part of our philosophy obviously to try to be friendly and try to be neighborly and bring those qualities into the brand and into how we approach those loyal customers, those friends of the brand. We are also working with a company right now to build out a more wide loyalty program where you can earn points and you can apply those points to purchases or where you refer a friend or you bring in X new customers for us that work for us and work for our brand. You're going to be rewarded with points, you're going to be rewarded with cashback options that are just going to make it worth your while because yeah, those are absolutely true. I mean, that's why I have punch cards to a couple of local Bozeman coffee shops and breweries. It's definitely a phenomenon that's here to stay. I don't know when loyalty was necessarily seized upon as an opportunity, but I can tell you it's definitely not going anywhere anytime soon. And it's something that as we definitely move into the DTC space even further, as we move away from third party wholesalers taking care of our business for us after they receive a delivery of whatever they've ordered, we're going to want to take ownership and get to know our customers better and make sure that they feel very happy and very taken care of.", "token_estimate": 535, "speakers": ["Mike Somerby"], "start_timestamp": "37:29", "end_timestamp": "37:29", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "886b0f37-0114-4eac-b190-8c0e5d4777af", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (39:58): Do you feel like that's crucial as you're making this transition away from wholesale?\n\nMike Somerby (40:05): Yeah, I definitely do. I think any brand in the direct to consumer base model rather should look at this loyalty and referral program as something pretty essential to bring into their mix. It's the way that, and I'm sure you've seen some of the information that the pandemic basically sped things up by a factor of, I mean, I've seen everything from two to 10 years in terms of the progress made in terms of the switch, the consumer mindset switch. It's kind of an arms race because of that in a lot of ways. I mean, everyone's competing for the same set of eyeballs on the same exact screen. It's not walking through a store, and it's like you kind of have someone's undivided attention when they're standing in front of your display. So I think adding on everything that makes sense for your brand aligns with your values. I can't think of any brand that wouldn't benefit or it would be against their values to have some sort of loyalty program, but I think it'd be pretty essential. And as we've built this ship, I mean, it's definitely something that we're going to lean into a lot harder.\n\nCole Heilborn (41:20): So I'm curious, does, I mean, I understand the loyalty program and how it could work, but I guess I'm questioning, you brought up the example of the coffee shop punch cart. Does that actually motivate you to want to go to that coffee shop, that punch card, or is it everything else and the punch card is just kind of like the icing on the cake and it sits in your wallet?", "token_estimate": 406, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Somerby"], "start_timestamp": "39:58", "end_timestamp": "41:20", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "74958629-3ed4-4b86-a171-6fea9cbb081d", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Mike Somerby (41:44): No, it definitely motivates me to go to that specific shop. I mean, it's kind of ironic because I'm not the biggest personal consumer. My spending habits might be different than what would be the average Americans. However, when I do have something like that, when I do have a punch card, it definitely incentivizes. I mean, I've got a place where I buy beer in town here in Bozeman, and I mean basically every 150 bucks I spend get basically a free six pack. So it's kind of a no brainer if I'm going to be buying beer anyway, if we'd be buying coffee anyway, I might as well be getting a good deal on it. And they're able, because of that shot bringing me in, they're able to create 10 times, it's 10 touch points that they otherwise may have not had. It's 10 opportunities that they've created to create a lasting impression on me to create some sort of loyalty to create a human moment where I get to know someone within that shop. (42:51): It's obviously a little different on a website, but when you do pepper out content and when you do have storytelling components and new video content or new product releases, every opportunity you can have someone to come back to your website in theory, is an opportunity to strengthen that bond. Whether or not they do purchase, maybe they just came in there just like, I'm going to take a look. How many points do I have? Oh, they got a new story out. Here's some crazy stuff from the ranch. Here's a new shirt that they didn't have before. And it's a window of opportunity.\n\nCole Heilborn (43:29): Yeah, no, interesting. We're running out of time for today, but as you look towards the future, what are you getting excited about? I mean, it sounds like there's plenty you could be excited about.", "token_estimate": 442, "speakers": ["Mike Somerby", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "41:44", "end_timestamp": "43:29", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "16f5f05d-7678-4e55-a150-fc718d4af229", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Mike Somerby (43:38): Yeah, I think this is the first year where all of our data, all of our information is really going to be directly related to direct to consumer. It's going to allow us to be a lot more informed, not to use this phrase because of where we're at in the world, but it was kind of a little bit of a wild west when we first went into the direct to consumer space. We're like, let's pull this lever. Let's push back on that. Let's try this, let's try that. And we're definitely whittling down to a much more efficient machine, which informs me as the marketer on how to best direct my time and best direct my resources, but it helps the rest of the team then make educated decisions as well. So we did have some data to work with. We did have some information that we could look to as a North Star, however, this is for real now we're actually building out stuff that we can look on for five years down the road and start building iterations of the current brand on top of what's going on right now. And I think to me, that's what I'm looking forward to most. It's, it was kind of a brand that had a lot of different fingerprints on it, and now it's really taking hold as its new form, and we're definitely starting to leave our mark and work within the existing framework to build something that works for the company as well. So that's what I'm most excited about, I think, is just being able to take advantage of that information and experience and really apply it to the betterment of Duckworth and everything that we believe in.\n\nCole Heilborn (45:25): Man. Beautiful. Well, this has been fun. I appreciate the deep dive into the history of the company, and I think, correct me if I'm wrong, but at some point in the future when COVID isn't affecting us, the sheep trail will be open to other people. Maybe", "token_estimate": 457, "speakers": ["Mike Somerby", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "43:38", "end_timestamp": "45:25", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "e67f8601-ff7b-4a16-8de3-111966f14b2b", "episode_id": "9ae8a864-5006-46ca-b5e0-006233234669", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4861", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Mike Somerby (45:40): It's something we're definitely toying around with. Speaking of loyalty is bringing some of our top customers on this experience because it's one of those things that you're going to tell the grandkids I'm going to. So I mean, it's just one of those things that we want to be able to bring more and more people into our world and show 'em what it's all about and leave them just with an amazing impression and a great memory. So yeah, that will definitely fit into our loyalty world coming soon, whenever we can get out and about without masks and all the shutdowns.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:20): Yeah, man, that's cool. That's got to be in a pretty incredible experience for anyone who gets to go on that. If people want to follow along with you with the brand, where can they go to connect?\n\nMike Somerby (46:32): Yeah, you can go to www.duckworthco.com. We have a Facebook and an Instagram that are very active, and both of those are just Duckworth co. So Facebook duckworth co instagram.com, duckworth co, and you'll find a lot of awesome content and really fun ways to see what's going on.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:57): Awesome. Sounds good, Mike. Well, thank you so much for your time, and I hope you have a good rest of your day.\n\nMike Somerby (47:01): Definitely, Cole. I appreciate being on. Thank you.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:04): Take care.\n\nMike Somerby (47:05): Yeah, bye.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:08): Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 407, "speakers": ["Mike Somerby", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "45:40", "end_timestamp": "47:08", "podcast_index": 60, "episode_name": "EP 60: Mike Somerby | Building Customer Loyalty | Duckworth", "slug": "ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-60-mike-somerby-building-customer-loyalty-duckworth", "guest_name": "Mike Somerby", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Duckworth", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mike Somerby, the Director of Marketing from Duckworth jumps on the mic to discuss storytelling and the intersection with customer loyalty.", "podcast_length": "44:82"}, {"id": "c1b364e7-1bb4-4e5b-ad0b-e48ac4b28e90", "episode_id": "d058428f-f662-4d1f-aa49-5ff24f16cac3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4862", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "David Zimberoff (00:04): We have really focused on recruiting great people and investing in those people, and if you do that and you empower those people with the tools that they need to get the job done in an environment that they're happy, it's the people that have gotten us through this.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:24): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Heur, and today I'm sitting down with David Zimber off the VP of Marketing, atra. David, how are you?\n\nDavid Zimberoff (01:05): Hey Cole. I'm great. Thanks so much. How are you doing?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:08): I'm doing fantastic. Super excited to be chatting with you today. Where are you joining us from?\n\nDavid Zimberoff (01:13): I am currently from my home office in Chicago, which is RA's global headquarters, working out of my basement as we're in this crazy time.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:24): Well, yeah, that seems to be where everyone seems to be hanging out these days. I'm curious to learn a little bit more about ra. I am sure many folks on this show who are listening are familiar with the company and the other companies that you guys manage. Can you share a little bit about strm but also your background? You've been with the company for quite some time now and have gone through many seasons of growth and change with it. Can you speak to that a little bit?", "token_estimate": 478, "speakers": ["David Zimberoff", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:04", "end_timestamp": "01:24", "podcast_index": 61, "episode_name": "EP 61: David Zimberoff | Leadership & Spending Brand Culture | SRAM", "slug": "ep-61-david-zimberoff-leadership-spending-brand-culture-sram-bb20e", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-61-david-zimberoff-leadership-spending-brand-culture-sram-bb20e", "guest_name": "David Zimberoff", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, SRAM", "categories": [], "short_description": "The challenges of leading a marketing team while working remotely", "podcast_length": "44:83"}, {"id": "5ded5a16-f86d-420b-af6f-5110e16dbecb", "episode_id": "d058428f-f662-4d1f-aa49-5ff24f16cac3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4862", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "David Zimberoff (01:50): Yeah, absolutely. I mean, big question, lots of questions there, but yeah, maybe I'll start first with Swm and its overview. For the listeners that aren't familiar, for those that are familiar, great. Welcome back, but for those that aren't, swm is a bicycle components manufacturer founded in 1987 around a product called Grip Shift. It's a rotational twist shifter. It kind of pioneered a twist shifting alternative in the late eighties, early nineties. Over time, SRAM has gone on to organically develop a bunch of products in the bike components industry, but then also make some strategic synergistic acquisitions. And so actually this week marks our 10th acquisition, which is pretty cool. Earlier this week we announced the acquisition of the Time Pedals brand, small boutique French pedal company, so we're super excited about that, but as I said, acquired a bunch of brands over time starting in, let's see, the late nineties with Sacks, a German bicycle components manufacturer in Germany and then followed in 2002 with Rock Shocks, great suspension brand. (03:10): A couple years later, 2004, we bought Avid hydraulic disc brakes, and then later that same year Truva, which brought us cranks, handlebar STEM C posts. A few years later we bought zip wheels in Indiana, high-end carbon fiber race wheels. A couple years later we bought a power meter brand called Cork, and then a few years later bought a small Kickstarter project called Shock. We've absorbed that into the Cork portfolio. Another year later, we bought an institutional power meter brand called Veron, sort of a collegiate indoor bike trainer, and then out of the CS group we bought PowerTap, and then just recently time, so 10 acquisitions over time. It's been a great role. As you said, I've been there for or here a long time, just past my 25th year atra actually this March, depending on when this airs will be my 26th year. So I've lived and breathed all the acquisitions and it's been an amazing, amazing opportunity. (04:22): I'm super fortunate. It's kind of a dream job for me. I didn't always start out in the marketing group. I started out, so sort of transitioning to my background, started out in industrial design. I was working for an industrial design consultant in Chicago, and Sarra was a client for a couple projects in the early to mid nineties, early nineties. And so Sarra was a client, worked on a couple projects together as a consultant, and then we fell in love. RA stole me away and I ended up being able to found the industrial design group in gosh, 95 is when I officially started inside atra, so created the industrial design group. It was me the first year and then within about a year, hired a couple more designers and then built out a team. We then bought our first company Sacks. I moved to Germany for a year and was part of figuring out what to do for ID there. (05:24): And through the years I'd taken on more and more marketing projects. I started to do the packaging and the advertising and trade shows and graphics and all kinds of marketing projects within the industrial design group. And I had such a passion for marketing as well that I convinced the organization to let me take over marketing. So I did. So 99, 2000, I took over the marketing group. I let go of industrial design, moved on from that. And yeah, now I run the global marketing group, which is about 60 people worldwide managing the whole portfolio of brands across the world.", "token_estimate": 872, "speakers": ["David Zimberoff"], "start_timestamp": "01:50", "end_timestamp": "01:50", "podcast_index": 61, "episode_name": "EP 61: David Zimberoff | Leadership & Spending Brand Culture | SRAM", "slug": "ep-61-david-zimberoff-leadership-spending-brand-culture-sram-bb20e", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-61-david-zimberoff-leadership-spending-brand-culture-sram-bb20e", "guest_name": "David Zimberoff", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, SRAM", "categories": [], "short_description": "The challenges of leading a marketing team while working remotely", "podcast_length": "44:83"}]
